1
00:00:01,300 --> 00:00:03,967
♪ In the not too
distant future ♪

2
00:00:03,967 --> 00:00:07,067
♪ Next Sunday A.D. ♪

3
00:00:07,067 --> 00:00:10,100
♪ There was a guy named Joel ♪

4
00:00:10,100 --> 00:00:13,433
♪ Not too different
from you or me ♪

5
00:00:13,433 --> 00:00:16,167
♪ He worked at
Gizmonic Institute ♪

6
00:00:16,167 --> 00:00:19,267
♪ Just another face
in red jump suit ♪

7
00:00:19,267 --> 00:00:22,433
♪ He did a good job
cleaning up the place ♪

8
00:00:22,433 --> 00:00:23,867
♪ But his bosses
didn't like him ♪

9
00:00:23,867 --> 00:00:28,867
♪ So they shot him into space ♪

10
00:00:29,767 --> 00:00:31,767
♪ We'll send him cheesy movies ♪

11
00:00:31,767 --> 00:00:34,033
♪ The worst we can find ♪

12
00:00:34,033 --> 00:00:35,367
♪ La la la ♪

13
00:00:35,367 --> 00:00:37,833
♪ He'll have to sit
and watch them all ♪

14
00:00:37,833 --> 00:00:40,167
♪ Then we'll monitor his mind ♪

15
00:00:40,167 --> 00:00:41,500
♪ La la la ♪

16
00:00:41,500 --> 00:00:43,933
♪ Now keep in mind
Joel can't control ♪

17
00:00:43,933 --> 00:00:46,400
♪ When the movies begin or end ♪

18
00:00:46,400 --> 00:00:47,667
♪ La la la ♪

19
00:00:47,667 --> 00:00:50,133
♪ Because he used
those special parts, ♪

20
00:00:50,133 --> 00:00:55,100
♪ To make his robot friends ♪

21
00:00:56,033 --> 00:00:57,433
♪ Robot roll call, let's go ♪

22
00:00:57,433 --> 00:00:58,300
♪ Cambot, pan left ♪

23
00:00:58,300 --> 00:00:59,533
♪ Gypsy, hi girls ♪

24
00:00:59,533 --> 00:01:01,000
♪ Tom Servo, hi tall guy ♪

25
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:02,933
♪ Crow, I'm the wise cracker ♪

26
00:01:02,933 --> 00:01:05,467
♪ If you're wondering
how he eats & breathes ♪

27
00:01:05,467 --> 00:01:07,900
♪ And other science facts ♪

28
00:01:07,900 --> 00:01:09,300
♪ La la la ♪

29
00:01:09,300 --> 00:01:11,600
♪ Then repeat to yourself
its just a show ♪

30
00:01:11,600 --> 00:01:14,967
♪ I should really just relax ♪

31
00:01:14,967 --> 00:01:19,967
♪ For Mystery Science
Theater 3000 ♪

32
00:01:23,433 --> 00:01:25,733
♪ In the Blue Ridge
Mountains of the-- ♪

33
00:01:25,733 --> 00:01:27,267
- Clay, Clay!

34
00:01:27,267 --> 00:01:29,300
I think I was spotted
on my way down here.

35
00:01:29,300 --> 00:01:30,700
- Did you wear your disguise?

36
00:01:30,700 --> 00:01:31,900
- I was wearing my disguise,

37
00:01:31,900 --> 00:01:33,633
but I'm just not
very good in heels.

38
00:01:33,633 --> 00:01:36,133
- No one must know we're
down here doing this.

39
00:01:36,133 --> 00:01:37,833
- I'm sorry.

40
00:01:37,833 --> 00:01:40,500
- Well, it's time to call
Joel about the experiment.

41
00:01:40,500 --> 00:01:42,967
Come in, Joely Poely,
pudding and pie.

42
00:01:42,967 --> 00:01:46,300
- Hey, sirs, I'm ready for
this week's invention exchange.

43
00:01:46,300 --> 00:01:47,433
Check this thing out.

44
00:01:47,433 --> 00:01:48,900
I just made it.

45
00:01:48,900 --> 00:01:51,767
It's the world's only
electric bagpipes, alright?

46
00:01:51,767 --> 00:01:54,900
(bagpipes screeching)

47
00:01:56,867 --> 00:02:01,300
♪ Amazing grace ♪

48
00:02:01,300 --> 00:02:04,467
♪ How sweet the sound ♪

49
00:02:06,567 --> 00:02:09,133
Okay, and the robots
and I have worked up

50
00:02:09,133 --> 00:02:11,767
a special cover version of Led
Zeppelin's "Whole Lotta Love"

51
00:02:11,767 --> 00:02:12,600
You ready, guys?

52
00:02:12,600 --> 00:02:13,533
- Ready.

53
00:02:13,533 --> 00:02:14,500
- Two, three,
- Rock it!

54
00:02:14,500 --> 00:02:16,800
♪ She's got a whole lotta love ♪

55
00:02:16,800 --> 00:02:21,033
♪ What a whole lotta love ♪

56
00:02:21,033 --> 00:02:23,767
♪ She's got a whole lotta love ♪

57
00:02:23,767 --> 00:02:27,833
♪ A really whole lotta love ♪

58
00:02:27,833 --> 00:02:28,967
- I love it.

59
00:02:28,967 --> 00:02:30,867
Look, Larry's
corneas are bleeding.

60
00:02:31,967 --> 00:02:33,600
Well, it's time we sent you

61
00:02:33,600 --> 00:02:36,000
our experimental
nugget this week, Joel.

62
00:02:36,000 --> 00:02:38,500
Now, human underarm perspiration

63
00:02:38,500 --> 00:02:40,833
is something that
happens to everyone

64
00:02:40,833 --> 00:02:42,133
after they go through puberty,

65
00:02:42,133 --> 00:02:44,567
which I assume
includes you, Joel.

66
00:02:44,567 --> 00:02:46,033
(chuckles)

67
00:02:46,033 --> 00:02:47,500
When's the last time
you saw a dog sweat?

68
00:02:47,500 --> 00:02:48,700
Larry?

69
00:02:48,700 --> 00:02:49,533
- Never!

70
00:02:49,533 --> 00:02:50,367
- Exactly.

71
00:02:50,367 --> 00:02:51,200
And why is that?

72
00:02:51,200 --> 00:02:52,200
- Dogs don't sweat.

73
00:02:52,200 --> 00:02:53,400
That's why.
- Exactly.

74
00:02:53,400 --> 00:02:55,433
Because of the
dog's pineal gland.

75
00:02:55,433 --> 00:02:58,033
Nature's own canine
antiperspirant.

76
00:02:58,033 --> 00:03:00,667
Now you take a pineal
gland and you make a serum.

77
00:03:00,667 --> 00:03:02,967
Get a dog, it doesn't
matter what kind of dog,

78
00:03:02,967 --> 00:03:06,700
and you inject that serum
into a human subject.

79
00:03:06,700 --> 00:03:08,867
In this case, Larry.
- Injections already?

80
00:03:09,833 --> 00:03:11,433
- Now, let's see.

81
00:03:11,433 --> 00:03:13,433
It's so hard to find
a spot I haven't hit.

82
00:03:13,433 --> 00:03:17,600
Uh, what's this flower
and who's Roseanne?

83
00:03:17,600 --> 00:03:19,200
- Oh, just stick it, will you?

84
00:03:19,200 --> 00:03:20,800
- Sail on, silver bird.

85
00:03:20,800 --> 00:03:22,333
- Oh!

86
00:03:22,333 --> 00:03:23,800
- Now,
- Geez!

87
00:03:23,800 --> 00:03:25,833
- Instantly the serum races
through the bloodstream

88
00:03:25,833 --> 00:03:27,800
like a Porsche Targa 911,

89
00:03:27,800 --> 00:03:31,567
commandeering each pore,
slamming it shut like the vault

90
00:03:31,567 --> 00:03:33,267
at your favorite
savings and loan.

91
00:03:34,200 --> 00:03:36,800
And checking the
wetness sensors,

92
00:03:36,800 --> 00:03:40,800
we see that they are free
from wetness and/or odor.

93
00:03:40,800 --> 00:03:43,467
(Larry panting)

94
00:03:45,100 --> 00:03:46,567
Oh, yes, the antidote.

95
00:03:46,567 --> 00:03:51,400
There you go, the antidote,
and here is your treat.

96
00:03:52,767 --> 00:03:53,933
(Larry chomping)

97
00:03:53,933 --> 00:03:55,367
- Oh brother!

98
00:03:55,367 --> 00:03:56,933
- That was pathetic.

99
00:03:56,933 --> 00:03:59,833
- Hey, no, I thought that
was really good, you guys.

100
00:03:59,833 --> 00:04:01,667
You're doing really
well, and I think

101
00:04:01,667 --> 00:04:04,667
that someday you'll be
ready for the Nobel Prize.

102
00:04:04,667 --> 00:04:05,933
- Maybe for fiction.

103
00:04:05,933 --> 00:04:08,267
- Hey, I noticed you moved.

104
00:04:08,267 --> 00:04:11,300
You guys must've got kicked
out of Gizmonic Institute

105
00:04:11,300 --> 00:04:14,067
for shooting us into
space like this, I bet.

106
00:04:14,067 --> 00:04:15,500
- Oh, don't be ridiculous.

107
00:04:15,500 --> 00:04:16,867
We moved.

108
00:04:16,867 --> 00:04:18,967
- It's our grand reopening.

109
00:04:18,967 --> 00:04:21,667
Welcome to Deep 13.

110
00:04:21,667 --> 00:04:23,200
- Deep 13, wait a minute.

111
00:04:23,200 --> 00:04:25,900
That's in the sub-basement
of Gizmonic Institute.

112
00:04:25,900 --> 00:04:28,400
I had to clean up a
flubber spill once there.

113
00:04:28,400 --> 00:04:30,400
It's incredibly radioactive.

114
00:04:30,400 --> 00:04:33,000
- But it hasn't
affected our brain any.

115
00:04:33,000 --> 00:04:34,233
- We like it here.

116
00:04:34,233 --> 00:04:37,300
Now we're even closer
to the atomic pile.

117
00:04:37,300 --> 00:04:39,233
And one day...

118
00:04:39,233 --> 00:04:40,967
- Well, I suppose
it's time for you guys

119
00:04:40,967 --> 00:04:43,100
to start experimenting
on us again.

120
00:04:43,100 --> 00:04:44,733
- I'll tell you when it's
time to do the movie,

121
00:04:44,733 --> 00:04:46,833
you squinty-eyed space chimp.

122
00:04:46,833 --> 00:04:47,867
- Oh, uh, Clay?

123
00:04:47,867 --> 00:04:48,967
- What?

124
00:04:48,967 --> 00:04:50,233
- It is time.

125
00:04:50,233 --> 00:04:51,567
- Oh, yeah, I knew--
- Nice insult, though.

126
00:04:51,567 --> 00:04:53,467
- I knew that, thank you.

127
00:04:53,467 --> 00:04:56,533
Well, it's a real stink burger
of a film this week, Joel.

128
00:04:56,533 --> 00:04:58,633
It's called "The Crawling Eye".

129
00:04:58,633 --> 00:05:01,133
- It's got a bad audio track.

130
00:05:01,133 --> 00:05:02,600
It's in black and white.

131
00:05:02,600 --> 00:05:05,533
And worst of all, it
stars Forrest Tucker.

132
00:05:05,533 --> 00:05:07,900
- Hmm, good name, bad actor.

133
00:05:07,900 --> 00:05:08,733
(laughs)

134
00:05:08,733 --> 00:05:09,967
I'll put in the tape.

135
00:05:09,967 --> 00:05:12,567
(men cackling)

136
00:05:14,700 --> 00:05:16,467
- Movies time!

137
00:05:16,467 --> 00:05:21,467
(doors clanging)
(metal creaking)

138
00:05:35,400 --> 00:05:37,633
- [Tom] This must be
a Paramount Picture.

139
00:05:37,633 --> 00:05:38,533
Pardon me.
- Yep.

140
00:05:40,467 --> 00:05:42,167
- [Joel] It's a
beautiful mountain.

141
00:05:43,200 --> 00:05:44,900
- Hey, Jimmy!

142
00:05:44,900 --> 00:05:46,700
- [Jimmy] Hang on a minute.

143
00:05:46,700 --> 00:05:48,100
Come see!

144
00:05:48,100 --> 00:05:49,700
- [Echo] Hey, Jimmy!

145
00:05:49,700 --> 00:05:51,433
- [Joel] Quit, quit Shouting!

146
00:05:53,333 --> 00:05:55,567
- [Jimmy] I tell you it's foggy.

147
00:05:55,567 --> 00:05:57,533
- [Joel] It's a mountain,
it's supposed to be cold.

148
00:05:57,533 --> 00:05:58,967
- [Jimmy] Wait a minute.

149
00:05:58,967 --> 00:06:00,567
Someone's coming.
- That's a bad Carey Grant

150
00:06:00,567 --> 00:06:01,600
right there, isn't it?
- Who is it Jim?

151
00:06:01,600 --> 00:06:03,333
The abominable snowman?

152
00:06:04,500 --> 00:06:06,733
- [Jimmy] I tell you,
there's someone coming.

153
00:06:07,867 --> 00:06:10,000
- Funny.
- Oh, what a good one.

154
00:06:10,000 --> 00:06:11,333
- [Jimmy] Who is it?

155
00:06:11,333 --> 00:06:12,567
- [Man] No, no!

156
00:06:13,533 --> 00:06:15,667
(screaming)

157
00:06:15,667 --> 00:06:16,667
- [Joel] Going down.

158
00:06:21,700 --> 00:06:23,100
Lemme help you there.

159
00:06:23,100 --> 00:06:25,700
- [Crow] Mountain
flossing really repels me.

160
00:06:25,700 --> 00:06:26,800
- Alright, now, pick it up.

161
00:06:26,800 --> 00:06:27,800
- Heave, ho.

162
00:06:28,800 --> 00:06:29,967
- [Tom] Heave.

163
00:06:31,433 --> 00:06:32,500
- Can you see him?

164
00:06:33,633 --> 00:06:36,100
- Not yet, he's
just below the lip.

165
00:06:36,100 --> 00:06:39,233
- [Joel] Aw, he got that
hat from Mike Nesmith.

166
00:06:39,233 --> 00:06:40,333
- Here he is.

167
00:06:40,333 --> 00:06:41,167
Jim.

168
00:06:42,267 --> 00:06:43,100
No!

169
00:06:44,233 --> 00:06:46,567
- Come on, come on!

170
00:06:48,433 --> 00:06:50,500
(grunts)

171
00:06:55,133 --> 00:06:56,267
You idiot, we nearly had him.

172
00:06:56,267 --> 00:06:57,667
Why did you let him go?

173
00:06:57,667 --> 00:06:58,333
- Didn't you see him?
- What are you talking about?

174
00:06:58,333 --> 00:07:00,567
- His head.

175
00:07:00,567 --> 00:07:02,500
It was torn off.

176
00:07:02,500 --> 00:07:04,600
- [Tom] You say that
like it's a bad thing.

177
00:07:06,800 --> 00:07:08,200
The Crawling Eye.

178
00:07:10,067 --> 00:07:12,033
Starring...

179
00:07:12,033 --> 00:07:13,600
- [Crow] It should
be the crawling me.

180
00:07:14,967 --> 00:07:17,233
- [Joel] Oh, Forrest Tucker,
he was from "F Troop".

181
00:07:18,433 --> 00:07:19,367
- [Crow] Hey, Joel,
how are you supposed

182
00:07:19,367 --> 00:07:21,267
to read these titles?

183
00:07:21,267 --> 00:07:22,833
- [Joel] Oh, well
Crow, it's simple,

184
00:07:22,833 --> 00:07:24,500
you see the line up there?

185
00:07:24,500 --> 00:07:25,700
- [Crow] Uh huh.

186
00:07:25,700 --> 00:07:26,967
- [Joel] That's, see
there's one there, see.

187
00:07:26,967 --> 00:07:28,200
- [Crow] Oh.

188
00:07:28,200 --> 00:07:29,267
- [Joel] You follow the
line and it shows you

189
00:07:29,267 --> 00:07:30,533
right what to look at.

190
00:07:30,533 --> 00:07:31,800
- We are here.
- Yeah.

191
00:07:35,400 --> 00:07:36,733
- [Joel] They quit
doing it 'cause people

192
00:07:36,733 --> 00:07:38,167
started figuring it out.

193
00:07:43,667 --> 00:07:45,867
- [Crow] Kinda like
titles with idiot clips.

194
00:07:45,867 --> 00:07:48,600
(dramatic music)

195
00:07:57,300 --> 00:07:58,567
- [Tom] Duncan Sutherland.

196
00:07:58,567 --> 00:08:00,767
Duncan, what a
yo-yo. (chuckling)

197
00:08:00,767 --> 00:08:02,300
- [Joel] Good one, Tom.

198
00:08:02,300 --> 00:08:04,467
- [Tom] Thank you so much.

199
00:08:08,100 --> 00:08:10,033
- [Joel] They're
the men responsible.

200
00:08:11,367 --> 00:08:14,133
(dramatic music)

201
00:08:15,100 --> 00:08:16,733
- [All] Directed by us.

202
00:08:23,433 --> 00:08:25,300
- Tempean.
- The light at the end

203
00:08:25,300 --> 00:08:26,167
of the credits.

204
00:08:27,767 --> 00:08:28,600
Ah.

205
00:08:33,833 --> 00:08:35,067
- [Tom] Let's see,
gemini, gemini,

206
00:08:35,067 --> 00:08:36,200
you'll be attracted
to a crawling eye.

207
00:08:36,200 --> 00:08:37,433
Leos figure prominently.

208
00:08:38,533 --> 00:08:39,533
- Anne?
- Hm.

209
00:08:40,967 --> 00:08:42,033
- Were you dreaming?

210
00:08:44,667 --> 00:08:46,500
- Oh, I don't think so.

211
00:08:46,500 --> 00:08:47,700
Why?

212
00:08:47,700 --> 00:08:49,367
- You were talking
in your sleep.

213
00:08:51,200 --> 00:08:52,567
- Did I give away any secrets?

214
00:08:52,567 --> 00:08:53,633
- [Crow] Just that you're a man.

215
00:08:53,633 --> 00:08:54,867
- [Sarah] Not really.

216
00:08:54,867 --> 00:08:58,367
I didn't quite catch his
last name. (chuckles)

217
00:08:59,400 --> 00:09:01,333
Would you like some tea?

218
00:09:01,333 --> 00:09:02,300
- No, thank you.

219
00:09:03,500 --> 00:09:04,867
- You sure you feel alright?

220
00:09:04,867 --> 00:09:05,733
- [Tom] What a babe.

221
00:09:05,733 --> 00:09:07,233
- I feel wonderful.

222
00:09:07,233 --> 00:09:09,367
I wish everyone would stop
treating me like an invalid.

223
00:09:09,367 --> 00:09:10,867
- [Joel] How about a wheelchair?

224
00:09:12,967 --> 00:09:15,367
- Look, Anne,
there's a mountain.

225
00:09:15,367 --> 00:09:16,300
- Ooh.
- Mm.

226
00:09:22,300 --> 00:09:25,367
- [Tom] I am Mount Svengali.

227
00:09:26,567 --> 00:09:28,767
You will do as I say.

228
00:09:29,767 --> 00:09:32,533
(dramatic music)

229
00:09:34,967 --> 00:09:35,933
Hey, my paper!

230
00:09:35,933 --> 00:09:36,867
What the--

231
00:09:36,867 --> 00:09:37,800
- [Crow] Hey, dibs, dibs.

232
00:09:37,800 --> 00:09:39,233
Everything on my lap I get.

233
00:09:42,533 --> 00:09:44,200
- What happened, did I faint?

234
00:09:44,200 --> 00:09:46,033
- Now don't worry, it's alright.

235
00:09:46,033 --> 00:09:47,767
- Is there anything I can do?

236
00:09:47,767 --> 00:09:50,700
- [Joel] You're Sergeant
O'Rourke from "F Troop".

237
00:09:50,700 --> 00:09:53,500
Oh, my name is Alan Brooks.

238
00:09:54,600 --> 00:09:57,167
- Oh, well I'm Anne Pilgrim

239
00:09:57,167 --> 00:09:59,800
and this is my sister Sarah.

240
00:09:59,800 --> 00:10:00,667
- Hello.

241
00:10:00,667 --> 00:10:01,500
- [Joel] Billy.

242
00:10:01,500 --> 00:10:02,833
- How do you do?

243
00:10:04,333 --> 00:10:05,400
There we are.

244
00:10:08,700 --> 00:10:10,100
- [Joel] That paper's shot.

245
00:10:10,100 --> 00:10:12,533
- I'm sorry about your
newspaper, Mr. Brooks.

246
00:10:12,533 --> 00:10:14,467
- Oh, that's alright, I've
got the baseball scores.

247
00:10:14,467 --> 00:10:16,533
There you are.
- It's just a prop anyway.

248
00:10:16,533 --> 00:10:17,767
That'll bring the color back.

249
00:10:17,767 --> 00:10:19,433
- [Tom] It's red
dye number seven.

250
00:10:22,667 --> 00:10:23,733
- Looks like you
might do with one.

251
00:10:23,733 --> 00:10:24,567
- No thanks.

252
00:10:26,500 --> 00:10:27,733
You have far to go?

253
00:10:27,733 --> 00:10:29,233
- Geneva.

254
00:10:29,233 --> 00:10:31,167
- [Joel] Oh, it's a big
convention town, isn't it?

255
00:10:32,900 --> 00:10:34,400
- [Man] Next Trullenburg.

256
00:10:34,400 --> 00:10:35,433
- Trollenberg?

257
00:10:35,433 --> 00:10:37,600
- [Tom] Yes, Trollenberg.

258
00:10:37,600 --> 00:10:39,300
- Sarah, we're getting
off at Trollenberg.

259
00:10:39,300 --> 00:10:41,000
- That's my stop.

260
00:10:41,000 --> 00:10:42,467
- What's the matter, and
you know we have to go

261
00:10:42,467 --> 00:10:43,500
on to Geneva.
- No, I really can't

262
00:10:43,500 --> 00:10:45,400
go on any further today.

263
00:10:45,400 --> 00:10:47,400
We can stop at the Hotel Europa.

264
00:10:47,400 --> 00:10:48,700
- What's the Europa?

265
00:10:48,700 --> 00:10:49,933
We've never been to this place.

266
00:10:49,933 --> 00:10:53,067
How do you know about
the Hotel Europa?

267
00:10:55,833 --> 00:10:57,700
- I don't know.

268
00:10:57,700 --> 00:10:59,433
- Anne, what's got into you?

269
00:11:01,200 --> 00:11:05,167
- We're getting off at
Trollenberg, please Sarah.

270
00:11:05,167 --> 00:11:06,567
- [Tom] Don't argue with me.

271
00:11:11,200 --> 00:11:12,767
Trollenberg, home
of the crawling eye.

272
00:11:12,767 --> 00:11:14,433
All stops lead to
a bloody death.

273
00:11:21,400 --> 00:11:23,100
- [Joel] It's Mr. Haney.

274
00:11:23,100 --> 00:11:24,633
- Oh, hey folks, sorry matey.

275
00:11:24,633 --> 00:11:25,900
Did you have a nice trip?

276
00:11:27,100 --> 00:11:28,567
- Hey Klein, nice
to see you again.

277
00:11:28,567 --> 00:11:31,000
Oh, I'd like you to meet
Miss Anne and Sarah Pilgrim.

278
00:11:31,000 --> 00:11:32,233
- [Crow] They'll drink anything.

279
00:11:32,233 --> 00:11:33,967
- He's the pride of
the Hotel Europa.

280
00:11:33,967 --> 00:11:35,367
Wonder if you could help us out.

281
00:11:35,367 --> 00:11:37,867
These young ladies have arrived
without any reservations

282
00:11:37,867 --> 00:11:39,600
but perhaps you
could put 'em up.

283
00:11:39,600 --> 00:11:42,067
- Yes, I guess it
would be alright.

284
00:11:42,067 --> 00:11:42,900
- Good.
- Thank you very much.

285
00:11:42,900 --> 00:11:44,100
- The car's outside.

286
00:11:46,133 --> 00:11:47,800
- Very good of you to put
us up at such short notice.

287
00:11:47,800 --> 00:11:51,367
- Well, this is not yet
our season, you understand.

288
00:11:51,367 --> 00:11:53,467
- Oh I thought now woulda
been about your busiest time.

289
00:11:53,467 --> 00:11:55,867
- Well normally yes, but--

290
00:11:55,867 --> 00:11:56,967
- [Tom] We've been having
trouble with the crawling eye.

291
00:11:56,967 --> 00:11:58,867
- Is Hans still working for ya?

292
00:11:58,867 --> 00:12:03,333
- Yes, Hans is still working
for us abroad, thank you.

293
00:12:05,900 --> 00:12:07,067
- Cigarette Sarah?

294
00:12:07,067 --> 00:12:08,000
- No thank you.

295
00:12:08,000 --> 00:12:09,500
- [Joel] Hey, look you guys.

296
00:12:09,500 --> 00:12:11,200
They're being
followed by a movie.

297
00:12:14,100 --> 00:12:15,033
- How 'bout you, Herr Klein?

298
00:12:15,033 --> 00:12:16,233
- Thank you, I don't smoke.

299
00:12:16,233 --> 00:12:17,200
- They shouldn't have
started the climb.

300
00:12:18,133 --> 00:12:19,500
- What are you talking about?

301
00:12:19,500 --> 00:12:21,000
- An accident.

302
00:12:21,000 --> 00:12:23,367
Last week, 12,000 feet
up the South Cull.

303
00:12:24,567 --> 00:12:26,867
Three English students,
one of them was killed.

304
00:12:28,533 --> 00:12:30,267
- Was there an
accident, Mr. Klein?

305
00:12:31,467 --> 00:12:34,900
- On the mountain, these
things sometimes happen.

306
00:12:36,067 --> 00:12:37,800
- What else do you know
about the Trollenberg?

307
00:12:37,800 --> 00:12:40,700
- Peasants are
leaving the mountain.

308
00:12:40,700 --> 00:12:41,933
They say it's bad luck.

309
00:12:43,233 --> 00:12:45,767
- The mountain people
are very simple.

310
00:12:45,767 --> 00:12:48,367
They are superstitious.

311
00:12:48,367 --> 00:12:52,367
Oh, all these
stories are nonsense.

312
00:12:52,367 --> 00:12:53,467
- What stories?

313
00:12:53,467 --> 00:12:55,000
- [Joel] Nonsense stories.

314
00:12:55,000 --> 00:12:57,467
- Disappearing into the
mist and never seen again.

315
00:12:59,567 --> 00:13:00,967
- [Crow] She is informed.

316
00:13:02,333 --> 00:13:04,400
- [Tom] When is Switzerland,
visit Trollenberg.

317
00:13:07,733 --> 00:13:09,400
- [Joel] It's
really picturesque.

318
00:13:11,533 --> 00:13:14,633
(light spooky music)

319
00:13:23,467 --> 00:13:25,367
- [Joel] What a
lovely mat painting.

320
00:13:29,433 --> 00:13:30,367
- Hans.

321
00:13:30,367 --> 00:13:31,633
(speaking foreign language)

322
00:13:31,633 --> 00:13:33,533
- [Crow] (speaking
foreign language)

323
00:13:33,533 --> 00:13:34,900
what's that supposed to mean?

324
00:13:34,900 --> 00:13:36,767
- [Tom] It means the
babes are coming.

325
00:13:36,767 --> 00:13:38,067
- [Crow] Oh!

326
00:13:38,067 --> 00:13:40,000
- [Tom] There they are.

327
00:13:42,333 --> 00:13:44,600
- Hello, you must be Mr. Brooks.

328
00:13:44,600 --> 00:13:46,267
Klein told us you were coming.

329
00:13:46,267 --> 00:13:47,867
I'm Truscott, Philip Truscott.

330
00:13:47,867 --> 00:13:49,367
- How are you Mr. Truscott?

331
00:13:49,367 --> 00:13:51,700
- Klein, you didn't tell us we
were expecting other guests.

332
00:13:51,700 --> 00:13:53,900
- Oh, this is Miss Sarah
Pilgrim and Miss Anne Pilgrim.

333
00:13:53,900 --> 00:13:54,867
- Hello.
- How do you do?

334
00:13:54,867 --> 00:13:56,200
- [Crow] They'll drink anything.

335
00:13:56,200 --> 00:13:57,433
- Sarah and Anne pilgrim.

336
00:13:58,867 --> 00:14:00,300
I'm sure we've met before.

337
00:14:00,300 --> 00:14:01,967
The name rings a bell.

338
00:14:01,967 --> 00:14:03,400
- Does it?

339
00:14:03,400 --> 00:14:04,967
Would you mind if we
went up to our rooms now?

340
00:14:07,900 --> 00:14:09,567
- [Joel] These
aren't my clothes.

341
00:14:11,567 --> 00:14:13,400
I don't look good
in stripes at all.

342
00:14:13,400 --> 00:14:16,133
(dramatic music)

343
00:14:17,300 --> 00:14:18,267
- [Crow] She could
eat corn on the cob

344
00:14:18,267 --> 00:14:19,400
through a picket fence.

345
00:14:21,533 --> 00:14:24,200
- Observatory at the
foot of the mountain.

346
00:14:24,200 --> 00:14:25,300
A cable car.

347
00:14:27,100 --> 00:14:28,367
There's a small hut
the climbers use.

348
00:14:28,367 --> 00:14:30,600
You can see it from
the observatory.

349
00:14:31,467 --> 00:14:34,433
It's all there, just like..

350
00:14:36,167 --> 00:14:38,900
- Just like what, Anne?

351
00:14:38,900 --> 00:14:40,467
- Well just like...

352
00:14:43,733 --> 00:14:46,267
Sarah, why did I
want to come here?

353
00:14:46,267 --> 00:14:47,700
What is it about this place?

354
00:14:47,700 --> 00:14:49,800
Why do I feel I've
seen it all before?

355
00:14:49,800 --> 00:14:51,600
- Don't worry about it darling.

356
00:14:51,600 --> 00:14:53,733
Lots of people get
a feeling like that.

357
00:14:53,733 --> 00:14:55,567
Perhaps you read
about it in a book.

358
00:14:55,567 --> 00:14:57,133
Or a picture in a travel folder.

359
00:14:57,133 --> 00:14:59,067
You mustn't let it upset you.

360
00:15:00,900 --> 00:15:01,933
- No I mustn't.

361
00:15:03,633 --> 00:15:06,767
Yes, you're right, I've
probably read about it.

362
00:15:06,767 --> 00:15:08,600
- [Tom] In the script.

363
00:15:11,367 --> 00:15:13,933
(upbeat music)

364
00:15:20,767 --> 00:15:22,267
- Hi.
- How are ya?

365
00:15:23,200 --> 00:15:24,400
- Need a hand?

366
00:15:24,400 --> 00:15:26,033
- No, I think I can manage.

367
00:15:29,300 --> 00:15:31,033
- Lovely spot, this, isn't it?

368
00:15:31,033 --> 00:15:32,267
- It certainly is.

369
00:15:32,267 --> 00:15:33,433
- You've been here
before, haven't you?

370
00:15:33,433 --> 00:15:35,433
- Yes, once, a
couple of years ago.

371
00:15:35,433 --> 00:15:37,833
- Good climbing.

372
00:15:37,833 --> 00:15:39,033
- So they tell me.

373
00:15:39,033 --> 00:15:40,333
- Don't you climb?

374
00:15:40,333 --> 00:15:41,867
- [Alan] Not if I can help it.

375
00:15:41,867 --> 00:15:43,000
- [Tom] Must be
his overnight gun.

376
00:15:43,000 --> 00:15:44,400
- [Philip] Come for holiday?

377
00:15:46,100 --> 00:15:46,933
With Herr Klein?

378
00:15:50,500 --> 00:15:52,067
- Yes, isn't it?

379
00:15:52,067 --> 00:15:53,167
- [Crow] Oh, this old thing?

380
00:15:53,167 --> 00:15:54,167
Saved my life once.

381
00:15:55,300 --> 00:15:56,900
- Attractive, those
two girls, aren't they?

382
00:15:56,900 --> 00:15:58,633
- Certainly are.

383
00:15:58,633 --> 00:15:59,467
- Pilgrim.

384
00:16:00,600 --> 00:16:01,400
Familiar name.

385
00:16:03,333 --> 00:16:06,200
I swear to you I've seen
them before, somewhere.

386
00:16:06,200 --> 00:16:07,300
On the palladium
about a month ago.

387
00:16:07,300 --> 00:16:09,400
They're a mind reading act.

388
00:16:11,700 --> 00:16:13,167
Funny they didn't say anything.

389
00:16:14,400 --> 00:16:17,667
- Well, perhaps they
wanted some privacy.

390
00:16:17,667 --> 00:16:18,967
- [Joel] Just like me.

391
00:16:20,100 --> 00:16:22,300
- I'll buy you a drink later.

392
00:16:22,300 --> 00:16:24,467
- All right, good.

393
00:16:24,467 --> 00:16:26,100
- [Tom] He knows
how to take a hint.

394
00:16:29,300 --> 00:16:30,600
- [Joel] He's kinda testy.

395
00:16:33,700 --> 00:16:35,767
Give me 06468 will you?

396
00:16:35,767 --> 00:16:38,500
(Crow squeaking)

397
00:16:43,067 --> 00:16:45,100
- [Philip] Yeah, I want you
to check on someone for me.

398
00:16:45,100 --> 00:16:46,233
(Crow squeaking)

399
00:16:46,233 --> 00:16:47,400
Alan Brooks.

400
00:16:47,400 --> 00:16:50,067
(Tom and Crow
speaking gibberish)

401
00:16:50,067 --> 00:16:53,100
He's American, he's
about 40 I think.

402
00:16:53,100 --> 00:16:54,567
- [Crow] Was he on F Troop?

403
00:16:54,567 --> 00:16:56,633
- [Philip] Try New York
first, and then Los Angeles

404
00:16:56,633 --> 00:16:57,567
and then Washington.

405
00:16:57,567 --> 00:16:59,033
- [Crow] Alright.

406
00:16:59,033 --> 00:16:59,867
- [Philip] Okay.

407
00:16:59,867 --> 00:17:00,767
- [Crow] Gotta go now.

408
00:17:00,767 --> 00:17:01,600
- [Philip] Right.

409
00:17:10,833 --> 00:17:13,067
- [Man] Hello there,
you'll be Brooks.

410
00:17:13,067 --> 00:17:14,000
- [Tom] If you want me to.

411
00:17:14,000 --> 00:17:15,800
- I'm Dewhurst, this is Brett.

412
00:17:15,800 --> 00:17:17,267
- [Crow] We're the hat brothers.

413
00:17:17,267 --> 00:17:18,567
- How do you do?

414
00:17:18,567 --> 00:17:19,900
Looks like you're
going for a climb.

415
00:17:19,900 --> 00:17:21,833
- Yes, we're going
up to Trullenberg.

416
00:17:21,833 --> 00:17:23,333
Gonna have a noggin
before we start.

417
00:17:23,333 --> 00:17:24,767
Care to join us?

418
00:17:24,767 --> 00:17:25,933
- Alright.

419
00:17:25,933 --> 00:17:27,467
- What's it gonna be?
- Scotch please.

420
00:17:27,467 --> 00:17:29,367
- [Dewhurst] Scotch
and a cool bandit.

421
00:17:30,733 --> 00:17:32,400
- [Crow] Nothing like a
little drinking and climbing.

422
00:17:32,400 --> 00:17:34,467
- Keep the cold out tonight.

423
00:17:34,467 --> 00:17:36,467
- You gonna stay the night?

424
00:17:36,467 --> 00:17:38,333
- No, there's a hut at the
foot of the South Cull.

425
00:17:38,333 --> 00:17:39,700
We'll sleep there
tonight and attack

426
00:17:39,700 --> 00:17:41,400
the mountain proper tomorrow.

427
00:17:41,400 --> 00:17:43,500
- [Crow] Unless it
attacks us first.

428
00:17:43,500 --> 00:17:44,767
- Hello Truscott.

429
00:17:44,767 --> 00:17:46,133
- [Tom] Who's the tomato?

430
00:17:46,133 --> 00:17:47,133
- [Dewhurst] You
must be Miss Pilgrim.

431
00:17:47,133 --> 00:17:48,367
- Yes, that's right.

432
00:17:48,367 --> 00:17:50,333
- Oh, this is Mr.
Dewhurst, Sarah.

433
00:17:50,333 --> 00:17:51,433
And Mr.--

434
00:17:51,433 --> 00:17:52,733
- Brett.
- Brett.

435
00:17:52,733 --> 00:17:54,567
- How do you do?
- would you care for a drink?

436
00:17:54,567 --> 00:17:56,400
I appear to be in the chair.

437
00:17:56,400 --> 00:17:57,933
Which is bon voyage.

438
00:17:57,933 --> 00:17:59,567
- Thank you, I'll
have a Campari.

439
00:17:59,567 --> 00:18:00,433
- Truscott?

440
00:18:00,433 --> 00:18:01,833
- Scotch, please.

441
00:18:01,833 --> 00:18:03,933
- Campari and another
scotch, Hans, please.

442
00:18:03,933 --> 00:18:05,200
- Sit down, Sarah.

443
00:18:05,200 --> 00:18:06,067
- Your sister's here
with you isn't she?

444
00:18:06,067 --> 00:18:07,000
Klein told us.
- Yes.

445
00:18:07,000 --> 00:18:08,233
- Would she care to join us?

446
00:18:08,233 --> 00:18:09,767
- No, not just now.

447
00:18:09,767 --> 00:18:11,767
- Quite an occasion for me.

448
00:18:11,767 --> 00:18:13,400
My first climb up a mountain.

449
00:18:13,400 --> 00:18:16,100
- [Crow] Aren't you kinda fat
to be climbing a mountain?

450
00:18:16,100 --> 00:18:18,400
- He's a big boy, he's
in the Alpine club.

451
00:18:18,400 --> 00:18:20,533
- How'd you come to
take up mountaineering?

452
00:18:20,533 --> 00:18:22,167
- Wonder if you could
come find some reason

453
00:18:22,167 --> 00:18:24,200
for all these accidents.

454
00:18:24,200 --> 00:18:25,567
I'm a geologist.

455
00:18:25,567 --> 00:18:28,567
I know all about rock
formations, that sort of thing.

456
00:18:28,567 --> 00:18:30,800
Well, here we are.

457
00:18:30,800 --> 00:18:31,967
Cheers.

458
00:18:31,967 --> 00:18:33,167
- To a good climb.

459
00:18:35,067 --> 00:18:36,500
- Keep an eye on
your rope, English.

460
00:18:36,500 --> 00:18:37,500
- [Tom] Huh?

461
00:18:38,800 --> 00:18:40,133
- Why rope in particularly?

462
00:18:41,400 --> 00:18:43,900
- I don't know.
- Two missing last week.

463
00:18:43,900 --> 00:18:46,133
- [Tom] Chill out.
- Nasty business, very nasty.

464
00:18:46,133 --> 00:18:48,000
- [Joel] Nasty, nasty.

465
00:18:48,000 --> 00:18:51,100
- Some kids climbing
on the South Cull

466
00:18:51,100 --> 00:18:52,633
and one of them fell.

467
00:18:52,633 --> 00:18:54,133
- They shouldn't have been
climbing without a guide.

468
00:18:54,133 --> 00:18:55,533
- [Tom] Or shoes.

469
00:18:55,533 --> 00:18:56,600
- Wasn't roped up
correctly and the rope got

470
00:18:56,600 --> 00:18:58,100
caught up around his neck.

471
00:18:58,100 --> 00:18:59,100
- Strangle him?

472
00:18:59,100 --> 00:19:00,800
- Worse than that.

473
00:19:00,800 --> 00:19:02,000
- [Crow] Killed him.

474
00:19:02,000 --> 00:19:03,100
- Tore his head off.

475
00:19:03,100 --> 00:19:05,200
- Even worse.
- Then is strangled him.

476
00:19:05,200 --> 00:19:06,500
- That isn't the half of it.

477
00:19:06,500 --> 00:19:07,800
- [Crow] It was the fall

478
00:19:07,800 --> 00:19:08,633
that killed him.
- What did the villagers say?

479
00:19:08,633 --> 00:19:11,533
- It's not for me to--

480
00:19:11,533 --> 00:19:12,833
- What do they say Hans?

481
00:19:12,833 --> 00:19:15,200
- [Tom] That's what they
say, "It's not for me".

482
00:19:15,200 --> 00:19:17,967
- The guides who found
him, also his friends.

483
00:19:19,533 --> 00:19:22,333
They swear the rope
was around his waist.

484
00:19:22,333 --> 00:19:25,600
You understand,
it was still tied.

485
00:19:26,867 --> 00:19:29,567
- But how could it have...

486
00:19:29,567 --> 00:19:32,700
- The villagers had something
to say about that, too.

487
00:19:32,700 --> 00:19:34,000
Haven't they, Hans.

488
00:19:34,000 --> 00:19:35,433
- [Joel] It was a trick knot.

489
00:19:37,033 --> 00:19:38,633
- Excuse me.

490
00:19:38,633 --> 00:19:41,633
(suspenseful music)

491
00:19:46,500 --> 00:19:47,867
- What do the villagers
say, Truscott.

492
00:19:47,867 --> 00:19:49,533
- [Crow] They say,
"It's not for me".

493
00:19:49,533 --> 00:19:50,467
- They say it that
it happened before.

494
00:19:50,467 --> 00:19:52,200
- [Tom] They all say that.

495
00:19:52,200 --> 00:19:54,400
- As I said, they shouldn't
allow any inexperienced

496
00:19:54,400 --> 00:19:56,600
climbers up the
Trullenberg without guides.

497
00:19:56,600 --> 00:19:58,133
There are bound to be accidents.

498
00:19:58,133 --> 00:19:59,467
- Yes, you watch your ruby.

499
00:20:00,833 --> 00:20:02,267
- We oughta be
moving if we wanna

500
00:20:02,267 --> 00:20:03,633
make the hut before nightfall.

501
00:20:03,633 --> 00:20:04,967
- [Tom] We've got a
death scene to make.

502
00:20:04,967 --> 00:20:07,300
- Oh, I'm going up
to the observatory.

503
00:20:07,300 --> 00:20:09,167
I'll hitch a ride with
you in the cable car.

504
00:20:09,167 --> 00:20:10,733
That is, unless you're
gonna climb the whole way.

505
00:20:10,733 --> 00:20:12,067
- Climb the whole way?

506
00:20:12,067 --> 00:20:13,100
Not if I can help it.

507
00:20:13,100 --> 00:20:15,867
(dramatic music)

508
00:20:17,600 --> 00:20:18,967
You see, there are
certain chemical changes

509
00:20:18,967 --> 00:20:21,200
that take place inside rocks.

510
00:20:21,200 --> 00:20:23,967
It's called a physical
alteration of its structure.

511
00:20:23,967 --> 00:20:25,833
At times it can
become like chalk.

512
00:20:25,833 --> 00:20:27,000
Break away in your hand.

513
00:20:27,000 --> 00:20:28,433
- What do you think
of that Brett?

514
00:20:28,433 --> 00:20:30,000
- Hm?

515
00:20:30,000 --> 00:20:32,000
Oh, I don't think
it's like that.

516
00:20:32,000 --> 00:20:33,500
A mountain's a mountain.

517
00:20:33,500 --> 00:20:35,067
Some people can climb
it and some can't.

518
00:20:35,067 --> 00:20:37,500
- [Tom] Some people can
speak dialog, some can't.

519
00:20:37,500 --> 00:20:39,300
Those who can't shouldn't try.

520
00:20:39,300 --> 00:20:41,667
I'm here out of sufferance.

521
00:20:41,667 --> 00:20:43,000
- How long you think it'll
take you to reach the hut

522
00:20:43,000 --> 00:20:44,333
from the observatory?

523
00:20:44,333 --> 00:20:45,633
- I don't know.

524
00:20:45,633 --> 00:20:47,233
- Oh, about three
and a half hours.

525
00:20:47,233 --> 00:20:48,500
It's an easy climb.

526
00:20:48,500 --> 00:20:49,933
- [Tom] That's good, I'm
wearing a sport coat.

527
00:20:54,433 --> 00:20:57,000
(wind blowing)

528
00:20:58,200 --> 00:21:01,733
- [Tom] Meanwhile,
at the observatory.

529
00:21:01,733 --> 00:21:02,733
- Professor.

530
00:21:03,700 --> 00:21:04,967
Professor.

531
00:21:04,967 --> 00:21:06,333
- How many more times
do I have to tell you,

532
00:21:06,333 --> 00:21:07,867
I am not to be interrupted.

533
00:21:07,867 --> 00:21:09,800
- I'm sorry sir, but there's
someone outside to see you.

534
00:21:09,800 --> 00:21:11,333
- I don't care,
tell him to go away.

535
00:21:11,333 --> 00:21:12,433
- He says his name is Brooks.

536
00:21:12,433 --> 00:21:13,867
- Tell him to--

537
00:21:13,867 --> 00:21:15,200
- [Tom And Crow] Huh?

538
00:21:15,200 --> 00:21:17,233
- Alan Brooks?
- Yes sir.

539
00:21:17,233 --> 00:21:19,100
- Well bring him
in, bring him in.

540
00:21:19,100 --> 00:21:19,967
No wait, wait.

541
00:21:20,867 --> 00:21:22,367
I'll bring him in myself.

542
00:21:22,367 --> 00:21:24,800
- [Crow] No wait, wait, you
get him, no, I'll get him,

543
00:21:24,800 --> 00:21:26,533
uh, yeah, I'll get him.

544
00:21:26,533 --> 00:21:28,833
I have to do
everything around here.

545
00:21:28,833 --> 00:21:31,233
- Alan, nice see you Alan.

546
00:21:31,233 --> 00:21:32,467
Ha-ha.

547
00:21:32,467 --> 00:21:34,267
They all get a smack for
leaving you out here.

548
00:21:34,267 --> 00:21:35,800
Come in, come in.

549
00:21:35,800 --> 00:21:37,433
Oh, it's good of you
to come and see me,

550
00:21:37,433 --> 00:21:38,800
and on you holidays, too.

551
00:21:38,800 --> 00:21:39,867
I was lucky it
was in Europe, no?

552
00:21:39,867 --> 00:21:41,533
You got my letter?
- Yes.

553
00:21:41,533 --> 00:21:43,667
- Well you don't say very much.

554
00:21:43,667 --> 00:21:45,133
What's the matter,
you're not pleased to see

555
00:21:45,133 --> 00:21:46,600
your old friend?

556
00:21:46,600 --> 00:21:47,433
- (speaking foreign
language) Herr Professor.

557
00:21:47,433 --> 00:21:49,033
- And how are you, Alan?

558
00:21:49,033 --> 00:21:51,933
Ha-ha, come in, come in, ha-ha.

559
00:21:51,933 --> 00:21:53,433
- [Joel] What a goof.

560
00:21:53,433 --> 00:21:57,000
- Well Alan, what do you think
of our little observatory?

561
00:21:57,000 --> 00:21:58,200
- [Joel] Groovy pad.

562
00:21:58,200 --> 00:22:00,233
- You know the
government gives me

563
00:22:00,233 --> 00:22:01,767
as much money as I want.

564
00:22:01,767 --> 00:22:03,267
(chuckling)

565
00:22:03,267 --> 00:22:05,033
- [Joel] Very retro, needs
some more color though.

566
00:22:05,033 --> 00:22:06,433
- I show you something.

567
00:22:09,133 --> 00:22:10,000
- A television?

568
00:22:10,000 --> 00:22:11,833
- Better than windows.

569
00:22:11,833 --> 00:22:12,667
So look.

570
00:22:15,800 --> 00:22:17,633
- [Tom] Look, the
all weather channel.

571
00:22:17,633 --> 00:22:20,167
These are mountains you won't
see on any other station.

572
00:22:20,167 --> 00:22:22,600
- You see, television
cameras on the roof.

573
00:22:22,600 --> 00:22:23,800
We watch everywhere.

574
00:22:28,167 --> 00:22:30,500
(chuckling)

575
00:22:32,600 --> 00:22:35,600
You know, the government, the
government, they say to me,

576
00:22:35,600 --> 00:22:39,267
"Professor, do you have to
have such expensive things?

577
00:22:39,267 --> 00:22:40,900
"Window are much
cheaper". (chuckling)

578
00:22:40,900 --> 00:22:45,000
And I say, "I have
to have", and I have.

579
00:22:45,000 --> 00:22:46,900
- [Joel] Do you
think that's funny?

580
00:22:46,900 --> 00:22:49,300
- Science humor.
- That is the only window.

581
00:22:49,300 --> 00:22:52,900
And even for that, we
have perfection, look.

582
00:22:54,133 --> 00:22:56,200
- [Crow] Now the windows
are even smaller.

583
00:22:57,100 --> 00:22:58,500
But I can make 'em big again.

584
00:22:59,633 --> 00:23:00,933
- [Joel] You could
just get roller blinds.

585
00:23:00,933 --> 00:23:02,533
- That will stand
up to any avalanche.

586
00:23:02,533 --> 00:23:04,767
(chuckling)

587
00:23:04,767 --> 00:23:06,767
but that too was very expensive.

588
00:23:07,633 --> 00:23:09,567
- [Tom] Ho, ho, ho, ho.

589
00:23:10,867 --> 00:23:13,467
- Well, Alan, come
into my office, huh?

590
00:23:15,067 --> 00:23:17,000
Well Alan, here we are.

591
00:23:19,000 --> 00:23:20,500
Same old mess, huh?

592
00:23:20,500 --> 00:23:23,033
- [Crow] But it's
an expensive mess.

593
00:23:23,033 --> 00:23:24,600
- [Professor] Well,
you're looking very fit.

594
00:23:26,833 --> 00:23:28,433
- I look the same as always.

595
00:23:28,433 --> 00:23:30,200
Come on, quit your
stalling, professor.

596
00:23:30,200 --> 00:23:31,733
You forget I know ya.

597
00:23:31,733 --> 00:23:34,500
You said in your letter that
I should see you right away.

598
00:23:34,500 --> 00:23:36,067
What's so urgent?

599
00:23:36,067 --> 00:23:37,567
- How long you been here
in Trullenberg, Alan?

600
00:23:37,567 --> 00:23:39,767
- Got here this morning,
came right up to see you.

601
00:23:39,767 --> 00:23:41,033
- [Tom] So you haven't

602
00:23:41,033 --> 00:23:41,967
tried the fish yet.
- You haven't heard

603
00:23:41,967 --> 00:23:43,133
about the accidents then?

604
00:23:43,133 --> 00:23:45,200
- Yes, I heard
about the students.

605
00:23:45,200 --> 00:23:47,500
- Yeah, that was
one of them, but,

606
00:23:47,500 --> 00:23:49,033
but there have been others.

607
00:23:49,033 --> 00:23:50,533
Many others.

608
00:23:50,533 --> 00:23:53,367
- Where people climb mountains
there are lots of accidents.

609
00:23:53,367 --> 00:23:57,700
- That's true, and sometimes,
the bodies, they disappear.

610
00:23:58,867 --> 00:24:01,667
But here, the search
parties go out,

611
00:24:01,667 --> 00:24:04,333
and always they find nothing.

612
00:24:04,333 --> 00:24:05,600
Now why is that?

613
00:24:05,600 --> 00:24:07,467
- [Tom] They're not
good search parties?

614
00:24:07,467 --> 00:24:09,000
- [Joel] But they're expensive.

615
00:24:09,867 --> 00:24:10,700
- I don't know.

616
00:24:12,733 --> 00:24:14,600
- And then there is the cloud.

617
00:24:14,600 --> 00:24:15,433
- What cloud?

618
00:24:16,867 --> 00:24:18,767
- Come on, Alan, you know
what I am talking about.

619
00:24:18,767 --> 00:24:20,300
The cloud where there
should be no cloud.

620
00:24:20,300 --> 00:24:22,033
- [Joel] Oh yeah,
oh yeah, that cloud.

621
00:24:22,033 --> 00:24:23,600
- Where there are mountains,
there are always clouds.

622
00:24:23,600 --> 00:24:25,333
- But this one remains static

623
00:24:25,333 --> 00:24:27,833
on the side of the
Trullenberg, it's never moved.

624
00:24:27,833 --> 00:24:29,033
- Let's go.
- Oh, right.

625
00:24:29,033 --> 00:24:31,500
- Hey.
- It's a freak of nature.

626
00:24:31,500 --> 00:24:34,400
- A radioactive freak of nature.

627
00:24:34,400 --> 00:24:35,767
- Radioactive?
- Mm hm.

628
00:24:36,867 --> 00:24:38,600
- Can we see it from here?

629
00:24:38,600 --> 00:24:39,700
- Come, I'll show you.

630
00:24:42,033 --> 00:24:45,867
(doors clanking and slamming)

631
00:24:50,033 --> 00:24:52,867
- Okay, what did you guys
think of the movie so far?

632
00:24:52,867 --> 00:24:54,800
- Well, I thought
there'd be more music.

633
00:24:54,800 --> 00:24:57,767
You know, more of a
Julie Andrews quality.

634
00:24:57,767 --> 00:24:58,867
- Julie Andrews quality?

635
00:24:58,867 --> 00:25:00,767
Isn't that a
contradiction in terms?

636
00:25:00,767 --> 00:25:04,367
- Now come on, Crow, what did
you think of the movie so far?

637
00:25:04,367 --> 00:25:06,533
- Well, Joel, I can't
understand why everybody's

638
00:25:06,533 --> 00:25:08,300
so upset about
losing their heads.

639
00:25:08,300 --> 00:25:09,800
What's so bad about that?

640
00:25:09,800 --> 00:25:13,033
I've seen Servo's head on
the workbench lots of times.

641
00:25:13,033 --> 00:25:14,867
- Yeah it screws right
off at the pop top.

642
00:25:14,867 --> 00:25:15,700
- Ew.
(popping)

643
00:25:15,700 --> 00:25:16,867
(laughing)

644
00:25:16,867 --> 00:25:18,300
- Well listen,
it's not like that.

645
00:25:18,300 --> 00:25:20,033
It's really, human beings
are made completely

646
00:25:20,033 --> 00:25:21,433
different you guys.

647
00:25:21,433 --> 00:25:23,700
Once our head are
separated from our bodies,

648
00:25:23,700 --> 00:25:25,500
that's pretty much the
end of the show for us.

649
00:25:25,500 --> 00:25:26,967
It's just the way we're made.

650
00:25:26,967 --> 00:25:28,933
- Can't you use your
back up copy and reboot?

651
00:25:28,933 --> 00:25:32,833
- No, nope, nothing like that.

652
00:25:32,833 --> 00:25:35,567
- Well then why do people
say that they're always like,

653
00:25:35,567 --> 00:25:38,000
"I'd lose my head if
it wasn't screwed on"?

654
00:25:38,000 --> 00:25:39,533
- Yeah, people often
say their heads

655
00:25:39,533 --> 00:25:41,000
aren't in the right places.

656
00:25:41,000 --> 00:25:42,767
- Yeah, and Joel, once I
heard the scientists talking

657
00:25:42,767 --> 00:25:44,867
and they said you had
your head up your a--

658
00:25:44,867 --> 00:25:49,133
- Well Crow, that was just
a figure of speech, alright?

659
00:25:49,133 --> 00:25:50,367
- Figure of speech?

660
00:25:50,367 --> 00:25:51,633
Now what's that
supposed to mean?

661
00:25:51,633 --> 00:25:53,533
- Oh, like a body of
a paragraph perhaps?

662
00:25:53,533 --> 00:25:55,433
- I think that's
a literary figure.

663
00:25:55,433 --> 00:25:58,000
- Oh, maybe like Edna
St. Vincent Millay?

664
00:25:58,000 --> 00:25:59,567
- Now there's a body.

665
00:25:59,567 --> 00:26:00,833
- Now listen you guys.

666
00:26:00,833 --> 00:26:03,900
I'm trying to teach
you something, alright?

667
00:26:03,900 --> 00:26:05,533
- Joel, what about head games?

668
00:26:05,533 --> 00:26:06,733
- And head trips.

669
00:26:06,733 --> 00:26:08,300
- And how do you
explain head cheese?

670
00:26:08,300 --> 00:26:11,500
- Well, head cheese, I
don't explain head cheese

671
00:26:11,500 --> 00:26:14,200
but here are some people who do.

672
00:26:14,200 --> 00:26:16,800
(upbeat music)

673
00:26:26,900 --> 00:26:28,400
- [Tom] Joel, will you carry
me everywhere from now on?

674
00:26:28,400 --> 00:26:30,500
- [Joel] Sure, I gotta put
wheels on you sometime.

675
00:26:30,500 --> 00:26:33,033
- [Crow] Hey, look, it's
that painting we saw earlier.

676
00:26:36,333 --> 00:26:38,000
- [Professor] Alan, come here.

677
00:26:38,000 --> 00:26:39,500
- [Tom] Hey, wait for me.

678
00:26:39,500 --> 00:26:41,067
Hey.

679
00:26:41,067 --> 00:26:41,900
- [Crow] Keep up.

680
00:26:44,033 --> 00:26:47,600
- Now you see, here's
a map of the area.

681
00:26:47,600 --> 00:26:49,067
- [Joel] Admittedly,
it's not a very good map.

682
00:26:49,067 --> 00:26:50,767
- [Alan] The Trullenberg.

683
00:26:50,767 --> 00:26:52,300
- [Joel] That's Trullenberg.

684
00:26:52,300 --> 00:26:54,200
- That is this camera
on top of the roof here.

685
00:26:54,200 --> 00:26:57,033
Now you see at the moment,
nothing, no reaction, nothing.

686
00:26:57,933 --> 00:27:00,100
But remember, here is the cloud.

687
00:27:01,667 --> 00:27:02,900
I turn on the scanner.

688
00:27:04,100 --> 00:27:05,500
Now watch when it
passes the cloud.

689
00:27:05,500 --> 00:27:06,633
- [Joel] Where's the cloud?

690
00:27:06,633 --> 00:27:08,233
Where's the cloud again?

691
00:27:09,933 --> 00:27:13,033
- Now you see it's passed
the cloud, nothing.

692
00:27:13,033 --> 00:27:14,000
Watch it go around again.

693
00:27:14,000 --> 00:27:16,000
- [Joel] Where's the cloud?

694
00:27:16,000 --> 00:27:16,833
Where is it?

695
00:27:17,967 --> 00:27:20,733
- [Tom] It's a cloud
of baked beans I think.

696
00:27:20,733 --> 00:27:22,633
- [Joel] Sounds
like it, right Tom?

697
00:27:22,633 --> 00:27:23,533
- [Tom] Sure does.

698
00:27:25,933 --> 00:27:27,900
- Well, what do
you think of that?

699
00:27:30,967 --> 00:27:34,833
Now Alan, I ask you to
think back three years ago

700
00:27:34,833 --> 00:27:36,933
to what happened in the Andes.

701
00:27:36,933 --> 00:27:39,533
- But, but this
can't be the same.

702
00:27:39,533 --> 00:27:40,467
- Why not?

703
00:27:40,467 --> 00:27:41,867
We have accidents like before,

704
00:27:41,867 --> 00:27:43,367
we have the cloud like before.

705
00:27:43,367 --> 00:27:44,533
Why can't it be the same?

706
00:27:44,533 --> 00:27:46,067
- Too many things missing.

707
00:27:46,067 --> 00:27:46,900
- [Crow] Like a plot.

708
00:27:46,900 --> 00:27:48,067
- A mental compulsion.

709
00:27:48,067 --> 00:27:49,200
- What is it?

710
00:27:51,233 --> 00:27:52,433
- The girl on the train.

711
00:27:53,867 --> 00:27:57,333
She suddenly decided she had
to get off at Trullenberg.

712
00:27:57,333 --> 00:27:58,633
She was booked for Geneva.

713
00:27:59,800 --> 00:28:02,767
But she had to get
off at Trullenberg.

714
00:28:02,767 --> 00:28:03,600
- And did she?

715
00:28:04,467 --> 00:28:05,467
- Yes.

716
00:28:05,467 --> 00:28:06,300
- [Tom] She had to.

717
00:28:06,300 --> 00:28:07,900
- She's at the hotel.

718
00:28:07,900 --> 00:28:11,833
- There's climbers
on the Trullenberg.

719
00:28:11,833 --> 00:28:13,033
- Climbers?

720
00:28:13,033 --> 00:28:14,600
I thought they were
all scared away.

721
00:28:15,800 --> 00:28:16,933
Where are they?

722
00:28:16,933 --> 00:28:18,767
- 16 degrees West of due North.

723
00:28:20,500 --> 00:28:23,133
- [Joel] Hey, it's "F Troop".

724
00:28:23,133 --> 00:28:25,467
(whistling)

725
00:28:25,467 --> 00:28:28,133
- [Professor] Improve our field.

726
00:28:32,600 --> 00:28:35,100
- [Tom] Two guys, hey.

727
00:28:35,100 --> 00:28:36,600
- [Crow] Captain Parmenter.

728
00:28:36,600 --> 00:28:38,400
- [Tom] Isn't he too fat
to be climbing a mountain.

729
00:28:40,000 --> 00:28:41,233
- [Alan] From the hotel.

730
00:28:41,233 --> 00:28:42,267
They came up with
me on the cable car.

731
00:28:46,500 --> 00:28:48,667
- Well, they should
be alright as long

732
00:28:48,667 --> 00:28:50,333
as they stick to
their present track.

733
00:28:50,333 --> 00:28:51,833
- [Crow] And they keep
their heads on their necks.

734
00:28:51,833 --> 00:28:54,133
- The cloud is well to
the west of their path.

735
00:28:56,033 --> 00:29:00,667
Alan, will you do something
about what I've told you?

736
00:29:00,667 --> 00:29:01,500
- The cloud?

737
00:29:01,500 --> 00:29:03,200
Do what?

738
00:29:03,200 --> 00:29:05,633
- Inform the authorities.

739
00:29:05,633 --> 00:29:07,633
- Look, I'm on a holiday.

740
00:29:07,633 --> 00:29:11,033
Besides you haven't told me
anything that can be proved.

741
00:29:11,033 --> 00:29:12,200
- I've told you about the cloud,

742
00:29:12,200 --> 00:29:13,067
I've told you about
the accidents.

743
00:29:13,067 --> 00:29:14,167
What more do you want?

744
00:29:14,167 --> 00:29:15,000
- Facts, proof.

745
00:29:15,000 --> 00:29:16,267
- [Joel] Scotch.

746
00:29:16,267 --> 00:29:17,067
- Something that'll look
real in black and white

747
00:29:17,067 --> 00:29:18,067
on the committee's desk.

748
00:29:19,467 --> 00:29:21,300
I don't know, I'm not gonna
stick my neck out again

749
00:29:21,300 --> 00:29:22,400
like I did in the Andes.

750
00:29:22,400 --> 00:29:23,900
- But why?

751
00:29:25,100 --> 00:29:27,400
You were employed by
the United Nations.

752
00:29:27,400 --> 00:29:28,600
It was all in the report.

753
00:29:28,600 --> 00:29:29,900
- Look, by the time the
Andes' report was in

754
00:29:29,900 --> 00:29:31,700
there wasn't anything
left in the area,

755
00:29:31,700 --> 00:29:33,300
explained or otherwise.

756
00:29:33,300 --> 00:29:36,200
They practically accuse me of
dreaming the whole thing up.

757
00:29:37,400 --> 00:29:39,300
If I was to take a hand
here, I'd have to have

758
00:29:39,300 --> 00:29:41,100
a list of documented facts

759
00:29:41,100 --> 00:29:43,200
and they'd have to
be pretty conclusive.

760
00:29:43,200 --> 00:29:44,133
- [Tom] Or I'd look
like a dork again.

761
00:29:44,133 --> 00:29:45,400
- You're an important man.

762
00:29:45,400 --> 00:29:47,067
Why don't you get
through the firm.

763
00:29:47,067 --> 00:29:49,867
- Politics, I'm only
important if I say something

764
00:29:49,867 --> 00:29:50,900
about cosmic rays.

765
00:29:52,333 --> 00:29:55,267
I say anything else, they tell
me to mind my own business.

766
00:29:55,267 --> 00:29:56,967
- [Tom] Why don't
they give him to study

767
00:29:56,967 --> 00:29:58,200
that he can pronounce?

768
00:29:58,200 --> 00:29:59,667
- Maybe you should
speak to Klein.

769
00:30:00,733 --> 00:30:01,967
- Hotel proprietor?

770
00:30:01,967 --> 00:30:03,900
- And he's also the
mayor of Trullenberg.

771
00:30:03,900 --> 00:30:05,667
Perhaps he could help.

772
00:30:05,667 --> 00:30:08,000
Anyway, he could supply
us of the accidents.

773
00:30:09,167 --> 00:30:10,433
- [Crow] He might
be more interesting.

774
00:30:10,433 --> 00:30:12,867
- Then perhaps you'll
do something yourself.

775
00:30:12,867 --> 00:30:14,967
- Huh?
(wind blowing)

776
00:30:14,967 --> 00:30:17,167
- [Joel] Meanwhile, back
at Daniel Boone's house.

777
00:30:19,100 --> 00:30:22,733
- [Tom] Damn, I'm just too
fat to be climbing a mountain.

778
00:30:22,733 --> 00:30:24,300
(chuckling)

779
00:30:24,300 --> 00:30:25,733
- [Crow] What a nice place.

780
00:30:25,733 --> 00:30:27,167
- [Joel] Gee,
there's only one bed.

781
00:30:27,167 --> 00:30:29,600
Guess we'll have to flip for it.

782
00:30:29,600 --> 00:30:31,667
- [Tom] He's too fat to flip.

783
00:30:31,667 --> 00:30:35,100
- [Crow] Hm, let me see, I could
kill the fat guy with this.

784
00:30:35,100 --> 00:30:36,133
No, too flimsy.

785
00:30:37,333 --> 00:30:39,600
Yeah, this is it,
nice and hefty.

786
00:30:39,600 --> 00:30:40,867
- You alright?

787
00:30:42,633 --> 00:30:44,000
Oh, take your pack off, man.

788
00:30:44,000 --> 00:30:45,267
You can't relax like that.

789
00:30:53,100 --> 00:30:54,067
- [Tom] I'll call
Weight Watchers.

790
00:30:54,067 --> 00:30:55,967
(phone ringing)

791
00:30:55,967 --> 00:30:56,767
- Hello?

792
00:30:58,200 --> 00:30:59,333
Yes, operator.

793
00:31:00,667 --> 00:31:01,467
You have arrived?

794
00:31:01,467 --> 00:31:02,600
- [Joel] Mm hm.

795
00:31:02,600 --> 00:31:03,667
- Good, good.

796
00:31:06,467 --> 00:31:08,033
A night's sleep
will rest your mind.

797
00:31:08,033 --> 00:31:10,833
(Crow imitating caller)
Yes, alright.

798
00:31:10,833 --> 00:31:12,467
- Okay.
- Goodbye.

799
00:31:12,467 --> 00:31:13,533
- [Crow] Thanks a lot, bye.

800
00:31:16,000 --> 00:31:19,700
- (speaking foreign language)
They have arrived at the hut.

801
00:31:19,700 --> 00:31:22,533
You see, you have
nothing to worry about.

802
00:31:22,533 --> 00:31:24,933
It is just unfortunate
that this year

803
00:31:24,933 --> 00:31:27,967
we have had many
inexperienced climbers.

804
00:31:27,967 --> 00:31:30,033
- And what about the
rumors in the village?

805
00:31:30,033 --> 00:31:32,700
- Well, you know these
people professor.

806
00:31:32,700 --> 00:31:34,567
They are superstitious.

807
00:31:34,567 --> 00:31:36,700
They like to believe
in fairy stories.

808
00:31:38,067 --> 00:31:40,767
Gentlemen, you
understand, officially,

809
00:31:41,700 --> 00:31:43,300
there's nothing we can do.

810
00:31:43,300 --> 00:31:45,300
- [Joel] Except sit
here and drink heavily.

811
00:31:46,733 --> 00:31:49,667
- [Crow] Dear diary, once
again the fat guy got the bed.

812
00:31:49,667 --> 00:31:50,767
- Until we come back tomorrow?

813
00:31:50,767 --> 00:31:52,033
If we travel light.

814
00:31:52,033 --> 00:31:53,900
- I must have a sack
for rock samples.

815
00:31:54,967 --> 00:31:58,233
- Oh, I think we
can manage that.

816
00:31:58,233 --> 00:32:00,167
You can take your sack.

817
00:32:01,000 --> 00:32:04,000
(suspenseful music)

818
00:32:06,400 --> 00:32:08,167
- [Joel] Penny
for your thoughts.

819
00:32:09,100 --> 00:32:10,700
Think we'll ever get married?

820
00:32:12,033 --> 00:32:14,933
- Visibility's is not too good.

821
00:32:14,933 --> 00:32:17,267
It'll clear.
- Fog, out here?

822
00:32:19,300 --> 00:32:21,900
- [Tom] Oh, it's just a
painting, sorry, my mistake.

823
00:32:24,667 --> 00:32:26,067
- Coming down form the peak.

824
00:32:29,000 --> 00:32:30,700
- Will this do, Miss Pilgrim?

825
00:32:30,700 --> 00:32:31,800
- Oh, this'll be fine.

826
00:32:35,233 --> 00:32:37,800
Quite a collection
you have there.

827
00:32:37,800 --> 00:32:39,533
Now would somebody
bring Anne in please?

828
00:32:39,533 --> 00:32:40,767
- Oh, yes.

829
00:32:40,767 --> 00:32:42,000
- Do we have to?
- Now, you'll see

830
00:32:42,000 --> 00:32:43,500
that I make no signals
to Anne and say nothing.

831
00:32:43,500 --> 00:32:46,000
So it's impossible for any
sort of code to be used.

832
00:32:49,933 --> 00:32:53,900
(Joel clears throat repeatedly)

833
00:33:00,200 --> 00:33:02,000
- The bank note?
(Joel grunts)

834
00:33:02,000 --> 00:33:04,367
- It's a mango.
- 500 Francs.

835
00:33:04,367 --> 00:33:06,700
- Mm hm, mm hm.
(clears throat)

836
00:33:06,700 --> 00:33:10,200
there's a number on it.
- Mm hm, mm hm, mm hm.

837
00:33:14,467 --> 00:33:17,333
- H-O-1
- mm mm, mm mm, mm mm.

838
00:33:19,667 --> 00:33:22,333
- 1-8-8
- mm hm, mm hm.

839
00:33:25,300 --> 00:33:28,400
- Well done.
(crowd murmuring)

840
00:33:28,400 --> 00:33:31,267
- And who does it
belong to, young lady?

841
00:33:31,267 --> 00:33:33,133
- [Joel] (coughing)
Perverts, robbers.

842
00:33:33,133 --> 00:33:35,833
- Well done.
(crowd murmuring)

843
00:33:35,833 --> 00:33:36,667
- Pretty good.
- well good, very done,

844
00:33:36,667 --> 00:33:37,500
very good.

845
00:33:39,267 --> 00:33:40,767
- A rounded sort of object.

846
00:33:40,767 --> 00:33:41,600
(robots grunting)
about the size

847
00:33:41,600 --> 00:33:43,267
of a tennis ball.

848
00:33:43,267 --> 00:33:44,433
Made of glass.

849
00:33:45,633 --> 00:33:48,000
I think it's used
as a paperweight.

850
00:33:49,967 --> 00:33:51,267
There's a model inside it.

851
00:33:53,100 --> 00:33:57,933
A mountain and a little
hut, and when you shake it,

852
00:33:57,933 --> 00:33:59,233
there's a snowstorm.

853
00:33:59,233 --> 00:34:01,300
- [Crow] When you shake
it, the heads come off.

854
00:34:01,300 --> 00:34:02,233
- [Tom] Rose bud.

855
00:34:05,767 --> 00:34:06,600
- No.

856
00:34:08,500 --> 00:34:13,500
Mountain, hut, two
men in the hut.

857
00:34:15,267 --> 00:34:16,500
- Anne.

858
00:34:16,500 --> 00:34:18,067
- [Crow] The fat
one takes the bed.

859
00:34:19,567 --> 00:34:21,233
- There's a fat
one, he's asleep.

860
00:34:22,667 --> 00:34:26,133
- In the bed.
- But he's not the one,

861
00:34:26,133 --> 00:34:31,000
the other one,
sitting at the table.

862
00:34:32,233 --> 00:34:34,033
- [Tom] Dreaming, doubting.

863
00:34:34,033 --> 00:34:35,667
- [Crow] Writing in his diary.

864
00:34:37,200 --> 00:34:38,133
- [Anne] He's the one.

865
00:34:43,667 --> 00:34:44,500
Getting up.

866
00:34:46,600 --> 00:34:48,367
Coming towards the door.

867
00:34:48,367 --> 00:34:49,400
- [Joel] Maybe
she's not psychic.

868
00:34:49,400 --> 00:34:50,567
Maybe she's controlling him.

869
00:34:50,567 --> 00:34:52,433
If she shuts up,
maybe he'll sit down.

870
00:34:53,967 --> 00:34:55,233
- [Anne] He's opening it.

871
00:34:58,833 --> 00:35:03,100
Coming out, up the slope.

872
00:35:03,100 --> 00:35:05,000
Up the slope.

873
00:35:05,000 --> 00:35:08,033
- [Joel] Flash, Mr. and Mrs.
America and all ships at sea.

874
00:35:08,033 --> 00:35:10,100
This is a banner extra,
front page stuff.

875
00:35:10,100 --> 00:35:11,467
Girl has vision.

876
00:35:14,100 --> 00:35:16,133
- Paperweights.

877
00:35:16,133 --> 00:35:17,733
Glass paperweights.

878
00:35:17,733 --> 00:35:19,100
(dramatic music)
- Whoa!

879
00:35:19,100 --> 00:35:20,333
Got her.
- Dudes, she's mine.

880
00:35:20,333 --> 00:35:21,267
I'll get her arm.
- I'm picking her up

881
00:35:21,267 --> 00:35:22,333
at her knees.

882
00:35:25,367 --> 00:35:28,033
(phone ringing)

883
00:35:35,433 --> 00:35:36,267
- [Dewhurst] Hello?

884
00:35:36,267 --> 00:35:37,800
- Hello, Brett?

885
00:35:37,800 --> 00:35:39,700
- [Dewhurst] Dewhurst
here, what do you want?

886
00:35:39,700 --> 00:35:41,133
- Are you all right?

887
00:35:41,133 --> 00:35:43,300
- [Dewhurst] Yes, why?

888
00:35:43,300 --> 00:35:44,400
- Is Brett there?

889
00:35:44,400 --> 00:35:45,900
- Of course he is.

890
00:35:45,900 --> 00:35:47,433
He won't go wandering off
in the middle of the night.

891
00:35:47,433 --> 00:35:48,867
- [Joel] Hm?

892
00:35:48,867 --> 00:35:51,767
- [Dewhurst] Wait
a minute, hold on.

893
00:35:51,767 --> 00:35:53,533
- [Joel] I'm fat, now
I'm fat and awake.

894
00:35:53,533 --> 00:35:57,367
He keeps calling me,
thanks for calling, jerk.

895
00:36:01,833 --> 00:36:03,167
- Dewhurst!

896
00:36:03,167 --> 00:36:05,100
Dewhurst, is Brett
there, can you hear me?

897
00:36:05,100 --> 00:36:07,000
Dewhurst, are you there?

898
00:36:07,000 --> 00:36:08,567
Hello, hello?

899
00:36:08,567 --> 00:36:09,833
- [Joel] Gonna have to
walk back over here.

900
00:36:09,833 --> 00:36:10,800
- [Crow] Could you
order me a pizza?

901
00:36:10,800 --> 00:36:12,200
- You're right, he's not here.

902
00:36:12,200 --> 00:36:13,867
He must've just gone out.

903
00:36:13,867 --> 00:36:14,933
I better go look for him.

904
00:36:14,933 --> 00:36:16,467
- No, don't do that.

905
00:36:16,467 --> 00:36:17,700
You'll be lost in five seconds.

906
00:36:17,700 --> 00:36:19,200
Just sit tight where you are.

907
00:36:19,200 --> 00:36:21,567
- I think I should go, there
may be something wrong.

908
00:36:21,567 --> 00:36:22,933
- No, you're not to move.

909
00:36:22,933 --> 00:36:23,867
Stay in the hut.

910
00:36:23,867 --> 00:36:24,833
What's the weather like?

911
00:36:24,833 --> 00:36:25,767
- Not very good.

912
00:36:25,767 --> 00:36:27,300
A heavy cloud coming down.

913
00:36:27,300 --> 00:36:28,733
- Well then stay where you are,

914
00:36:28,733 --> 00:36:30,300
there'll be somebody listening
to the phone all night.

915
00:36:30,300 --> 00:36:31,833
If Brett shows up
or if you need us,

916
00:36:31,833 --> 00:36:33,133
ring us here, will ya?

917
00:36:33,133 --> 00:36:34,300
- All right.

918
00:36:34,300 --> 00:36:35,333
- Bye.
- Bye.

919
00:36:41,467 --> 00:36:43,933
- [Crow] Well, let's see,
something to hold me over.

920
00:36:45,400 --> 00:36:47,967
Eh, well, I can't eat that,
it'll just make me hungrier.

921
00:36:49,900 --> 00:36:50,800
Maybe the blanket.

922
00:36:53,833 --> 00:36:57,400
(wind blowing)
- Brett!

923
00:36:57,400 --> 00:37:01,833
Brett! (echoing)

924
00:37:07,733 --> 00:37:10,233
(door closes)

925
00:37:11,433 --> 00:37:14,800
(man speaking foreign language)

926
00:37:14,800 --> 00:37:18,500
- [Joel] It was a bizarre
dream and you were all there.

927
00:37:18,500 --> 00:37:19,767
- Where am I, what happened?

928
00:37:19,767 --> 00:37:21,733
- [Joel] Fanny Flag and
Groucho and Carl Sagan.

929
00:37:21,733 --> 00:37:24,533
And it was a Dick
Cavett PBS special.

930
00:37:24,533 --> 00:37:27,100
- Well, we need something
to relax your sister.

931
00:37:27,100 --> 00:37:28,933
A sedative perhaps, do
you have such a thing?

932
00:37:28,933 --> 00:37:30,767
- I may have some
sleeping pills in my room.

933
00:37:30,767 --> 00:37:31,633
Shall I see if I can find them?

934
00:37:31,633 --> 00:37:33,167
- Oh, please, thank you.

935
00:37:35,867 --> 00:37:39,200
(door opens and closes)

936
00:37:41,500 --> 00:37:44,333
- Sarah, may I speak with
you for a minute please?

937
00:37:46,867 --> 00:37:47,700
- Alright.

938
00:37:49,433 --> 00:37:51,233
(squeaking)

939
00:37:51,233 --> 00:37:52,167
I shan't be a minute.

940
00:37:52,167 --> 00:37:53,933
- [Joel] What' that noise.

941
00:37:53,933 --> 00:37:56,133
- [Tom] I think
somebody's sharpening
a pencil or something.

942
00:37:56,133 --> 00:37:58,200
- [Crow] I think it's
the electric blanket.

943
00:37:58,200 --> 00:38:01,133
(squeaking static)

944
00:38:04,067 --> 00:38:06,833
- Now, Miss Sarah, I don't
want you to upset yourself.

945
00:38:06,833 --> 00:38:08,000
- Are these any good?

946
00:38:08,000 --> 00:38:10,100
- [Joel] He prescribed
those for Elvis.

947
00:38:10,100 --> 00:38:11,700
- Yes, it's fine.

948
00:38:11,700 --> 00:38:13,467
Now you give her two of these,

949
00:38:13,467 --> 00:38:15,767
then come downstairs,
please Sarah.

950
00:38:15,767 --> 00:38:16,600
- Alright.

951
00:38:21,867 --> 00:38:23,200
- [Crow] Is it my breath?

952
00:38:23,200 --> 00:38:25,767
(upbeat music)

953
00:38:30,600 --> 00:38:33,267
(phone ringing)

954
00:38:34,300 --> 00:38:35,533
- Hello?

955
00:38:35,533 --> 00:38:36,667
- [Tom] My pizza ready?

956
00:38:37,733 --> 00:38:38,833
Been over a half hour.

957
00:38:40,333 --> 00:38:42,933
It's foggy as hell outside,
you know I can't see far.

958
00:38:42,933 --> 00:38:44,500
Perishing cold, too.

959
00:38:45,800 --> 00:38:47,167
Yes.

960
00:38:47,167 --> 00:38:48,000
Yes.

961
00:38:49,067 --> 00:38:50,167
Hold on a minute.

962
00:38:51,333 --> 00:38:53,033
There's someone
outside, hang on.

963
00:38:53,033 --> 00:38:54,933
- [Joel] Maybe it's
the neighbor kids.

964
00:38:54,933 --> 00:38:56,167
- [Crow] Could be the pizza man.

965
00:38:56,167 --> 00:38:57,700
- [Tom] Hey, get
outta my flowers.

966
00:38:58,900 --> 00:39:01,033
- [Crow] It's the
stealth pizza man.

967
00:39:01,033 --> 00:39:02,867
- Brett, Brett is that you?

968
00:39:03,700 --> 00:39:06,433
(dramatic music)

969
00:39:08,400 --> 00:39:09,633
False alarm, I guess.

970
00:39:12,800 --> 00:39:14,300
Hang on a minute, I'm
just gonna close the door.

971
00:39:21,500 --> 00:39:24,200
(screams)

972
00:39:24,200 --> 00:39:26,133
- [Joel] It's the noid.

973
00:39:27,533 --> 00:39:29,667
- [Tom] Do we ever get
to see the monster, Joel?

974
00:39:29,667 --> 00:39:32,167
- [Joel] I think they're sick.

975
00:39:34,167 --> 00:39:37,000
- Dewhurst, hello, hello.

976
00:39:37,000 --> 00:39:40,667
Dewhurst, are you there?
(screaming)

977
00:39:40,667 --> 00:39:42,033
- [Crow] Anchovies.

978
00:39:42,033 --> 00:39:42,967
- Well?

979
00:39:42,967 --> 00:39:43,800
- [Crow] He said anchovies.

980
00:39:43,800 --> 00:39:45,000
- The phone's gone dead.

981
00:39:46,500 --> 00:39:49,167
(phone ringing)

982
00:39:49,167 --> 00:39:50,733
- [Tom] That one hasn't.

983
00:39:50,733 --> 00:39:51,867
- Hello?

984
00:39:52,967 --> 00:39:53,800
Yeah, speaking.

985
00:39:55,067 --> 00:39:56,000
Yes.

986
00:39:56,000 --> 00:39:56,967
- [Tom] It's Harpo.

987
00:39:58,033 --> 00:39:59,100
- I see.

988
00:39:59,100 --> 00:39:59,933
- [Joel] Hm?

989
00:40:00,867 --> 00:40:02,900
- Alright, alright.

990
00:40:04,533 --> 00:40:05,600
- [Joel] He said anchovies.

991
00:40:08,700 --> 00:40:10,733
- It was the observatory.

992
00:40:10,733 --> 00:40:13,033
The cloud has moved
away from the hut.

993
00:40:14,200 --> 00:40:16,133
Back out to Trullenberg.

994
00:40:16,133 --> 00:40:18,367
- [Joel And Robots]
Hey, nice real change.

995
00:40:18,367 --> 00:40:19,700
- We must get back Herr Horden.

996
00:40:19,700 --> 00:40:20,900
- Jawohl.

997
00:40:20,900 --> 00:40:22,067
- [Crow] You think this
outfit is too busy?

998
00:40:22,067 --> 00:40:25,800
(speaking foreign language)
- Yah.

999
00:40:25,800 --> 00:40:28,033
- We better start now,
it will be light soon.

1000
00:40:31,000 --> 00:40:32,000
- I thought the
locals were afraid

1001
00:40:32,000 --> 00:40:33,533
of going up that mountain.

1002
00:40:33,533 --> 00:40:34,933
- They are afraid.

1003
00:40:34,933 --> 00:40:36,233
There's an unwritten law here.

1004
00:40:36,233 --> 00:40:38,900
A time like this, they
go up no matter what.

1005
00:40:38,900 --> 00:40:40,200
- [Joel] Even without pants.

1006
00:40:40,200 --> 00:40:42,733
- I have called a charter plane,

1007
00:40:42,733 --> 00:40:44,367
but it will not
be up til morning.

1008
00:40:44,367 --> 00:40:46,600
We will be well on
our way by then.

1009
00:40:46,600 --> 00:40:48,100
Ready?
- All right, Herr Klein.

1010
00:40:48,100 --> 00:40:50,567
- [Tom] Should I have my glasses
on or off for this climb?

1011
00:40:50,567 --> 00:40:52,433
- Look after yourself Alan.

1012
00:40:52,433 --> 00:40:54,600
- Don't worry, I'll
stop by the observatory

1013
00:40:54,600 --> 00:40:55,800
on the way down.
- Good.

1014
00:40:56,733 --> 00:40:57,867
Be careful everybody.

1015
00:40:59,833 --> 00:41:01,300
- [Crow] Don't lose you heads.

1016
00:41:02,700 --> 00:41:04,667
- [Tom] These four brave men,

1017
00:41:04,667 --> 00:41:06,200
(screaming)

1018
00:41:06,200 --> 00:41:07,533
