so, Miss Pilgrim.

1263
00:51:33,900 --> 00:51:36,967
I think you ought to wait
a while at the observatory.

1264
00:51:36,967 --> 00:51:39,433
- [Joel] What color is
the sky in your world?

1265
00:51:39,433 --> 00:51:41,267
(whistling)

1266
00:51:41,267 --> 00:51:43,567
- [Tom] Just about,
almost, almost (grunting).

1267
00:51:46,400 --> 00:51:48,867
(speaking foreign language)

1268
00:51:48,867 --> 00:51:50,267
- [Joel] What?

1269
00:51:50,267 --> 00:51:52,100
- [Tom] He said,
here's a bloody bag.

1270
00:52:03,767 --> 00:52:05,167
Better have a look-see.

1271
00:52:05,167 --> 00:52:08,100
- Herr Brett! (echoing)

1272
00:52:08,100 --> 00:52:09,267
- [Joel] Echo!

1273
00:52:10,367 --> 00:52:11,500
- [Crow] Needs a shave.

1274
00:52:14,533 --> 00:52:15,633
- [Tom] What?

1275
00:52:16,933 --> 00:52:18,167
Pudding?

1276
00:52:18,167 --> 00:52:19,733
- [Joel] Maybe it's
somebody's lunch.

1277
00:52:19,733 --> 00:52:22,167
- Hey, (speaking
foreign language).

1278
00:52:22,167 --> 00:52:23,900
- [Tom] Coming see here schnell?

1279
00:52:24,767 --> 00:52:26,167
- [Crow] Alright, 'salright.

1280
00:52:27,033 --> 00:52:28,767
- [Tom] They found Hoffa.

1281
00:52:28,767 --> 00:52:31,367
You've looked in my bag!

1282
00:52:31,367 --> 00:52:34,067
- [Crow] Hey, buddy, it won't
get better if you pick at it.

1283
00:52:35,967 --> 00:52:37,200
- [Joel] Yeah, your
friend, I got a bone

1284
00:52:37,200 --> 00:52:38,667
to pick with you too.

1285
00:52:38,667 --> 00:52:40,433
- [Tom] I suppose you
wanna look in my bag too!

1286
00:52:40,433 --> 00:52:42,267
- [Joel] And it's yours.

1287
00:52:42,267 --> 00:52:43,667
- [Crow] That's gotta hurt.

1288
00:52:43,667 --> 00:52:48,667
(speaking foreign language)
(machine whirring)

1289
00:52:49,400 --> 00:52:50,533
- How 'bout that?

1290
00:52:50,533 --> 00:52:51,300
- Negative.

1291
00:52:53,267 --> 00:52:54,567
- Hugo?
- Negative.

1292
00:52:59,033 --> 00:53:00,733
- Bring it over 30 degrees.

1293
00:53:00,733 --> 00:53:04,100
(machine whirring)

1294
00:53:04,100 --> 00:53:05,233
- Negative.

1295
00:53:05,233 --> 00:53:06,400
- [Joel] Everybody's
so negative.

1296
00:53:10,633 --> 00:53:12,633
- Try the other circuit.

1297
00:53:13,700 --> 00:53:15,200
Oh, wait.

1298
00:53:15,200 --> 00:53:19,867
Switch it back to the first
circuit and reverse the scan.

1299
00:53:21,333 --> 00:53:23,667
- [Tom] This'll never
replace Nintendo.

1300
00:53:23,667 --> 00:53:24,900
- [Crow] Now's my
chance to beat it.

1301
00:53:24,900 --> 00:53:27,167
- [Tom] I hope I don't
run into Forrest Tucker.

1302
00:53:28,967 --> 00:53:30,100
Huh?

1303
00:53:30,100 --> 00:53:31,200
- Oh, hello Anne.

1304
00:53:31,200 --> 00:53:32,567
What are you doing up here?

1305
00:53:33,467 --> 00:53:34,900
- [Crow] Uh, nothing.

1306
00:53:34,900 --> 00:53:36,400
- [Joel] Probably waiting
for the commercial.

1307
00:53:36,400 --> 00:53:38,233
- Go, go where?

1308
00:53:38,233 --> 00:53:41,133
- To Trullenberg,
I must go, now.

1309
00:53:41,133 --> 00:53:42,433
- [Professor] Don't
let that girl go.

1310
00:53:42,433 --> 00:53:44,167
- Don't worry.

1311
00:53:44,167 --> 00:53:45,400
Come along now.

1312
00:53:51,633 --> 00:53:54,067
- Oh, good, Alan I
want to speak with you.

1313
00:53:54,067 --> 00:53:56,100
- Yeah, I got news for you too.

1314
00:53:56,100 --> 00:53:58,567
When does the next cable
car go down the mountain?

1315
00:53:58,567 --> 00:54:00,700
- It shouldn't be long, sir.

1316
00:54:00,700 --> 00:54:02,567
- Good I, I want you
to take Anne down,

1317
00:54:02,567 --> 00:54:04,733
don't let her outta your sight.

1318
00:54:04,733 --> 00:54:07,367
Anne, I want you
to go with Hans.

1319
00:54:07,367 --> 00:54:08,333
- [Joel] He's a warm
and wonderful man,

1320
00:54:08,333 --> 00:54:10,033
you will be very happy.

1321
00:54:11,100 --> 00:54:12,433
- [Tom] Groping Hans.

1322
00:54:14,567 --> 00:54:16,400
- [Crow] Hey everybody,
I got a girl.

1323
00:54:17,600 --> 00:54:19,133
They make a lovely
couple don't they?

1324
00:54:24,133 --> 00:54:26,067
- Well, I've been to the hut.

1325
00:54:26,067 --> 00:54:27,267
- And?

1326
00:54:27,267 --> 00:54:29,800
- Dewhurst is dead,
Brett's missing.

1327
00:54:31,667 --> 00:54:35,633
- Oy, not that I'm
surprised, mind you.

1328
00:54:36,867 --> 00:54:38,700
The cloud has
started moving again.

1329
00:54:40,467 --> 00:54:41,667
- What cloud?

1330
00:54:41,667 --> 00:54:42,900
- [Tom] Bozo the cloud.

1331
00:54:42,900 --> 00:54:45,200
- I don't think you'd
understand, Philip.

1332
00:54:45,200 --> 00:54:46,367
- [Crow] You're much too stupid.

1333
00:54:46,367 --> 00:54:48,233
- Like that time in the Andes?

1334
00:54:48,233 --> 00:54:49,600
- Hm?
- Doh.

1335
00:54:49,600 --> 00:54:51,300
- How'd you know about that?

1336
00:54:51,300 --> 00:54:52,567
- [Joel] Oh, I get around.

1337
00:54:52,567 --> 00:54:54,800
- Alright, I'm a newspaper man,

1338
00:54:54,800 --> 00:54:55,900
it's my job to know

1339
00:54:55,900 --> 00:54:58,500
about these things.
(groaning)

1340
00:54:58,500 --> 00:54:59,333
- I see.

1341
00:55:00,533 --> 00:55:02,200
- I chased that story
when we got a wind

1342
00:55:02,200 --> 00:55:04,000
of your report, Alan.

1343
00:55:04,000 --> 00:55:05,667
When I got there,
it was too late.

1344
00:55:05,667 --> 00:55:07,300
Looks as if I'm gonna be

1345
00:55:07,300 --> 00:55:09,433
more fortunate this
time, doesn't it?

1346
00:55:09,433 --> 00:55:12,800
- Yeah, fine, fine,
that's just what we need.

1347
00:55:15,067 --> 00:55:17,267
You haven't answered
my question, Professor.

1348
00:55:19,300 --> 00:55:20,533
- What question?

1349
00:55:20,533 --> 00:55:22,733
- Is this the same
as it was then?

1350
00:55:22,733 --> 00:55:25,367
- [Crow] Well, that was
then and this is now.

1351
00:55:25,367 --> 00:55:26,767
- So what happens now?

1352
00:55:26,767 --> 00:55:28,033
- [Joel] This.

1353
00:55:28,033 --> 00:55:29,700
- The United Nations
have a special team

1354
00:55:29,700 --> 00:55:31,800
for investigating phenomena.

1355
00:55:31,800 --> 00:55:33,467
Might call 'em in.

1356
00:55:33,467 --> 00:55:35,633
- [Tom] They also double
as our softball team.

1357
00:55:35,633 --> 00:55:37,600
- The theory that you
put forward last time

1358
00:55:37,600 --> 00:55:38,700
still hold, Professor?

1359
00:55:38,700 --> 00:55:40,167
- What theory?

1360
00:55:40,167 --> 00:55:43,033
- Visitors from outer space,
that was it, wasn't it?

1361
00:55:43,033 --> 00:55:45,200
- Well, what else?
- Well, what are they?

1362
00:55:45,200 --> 00:55:46,633
What do they want?

1363
00:55:46,633 --> 00:55:48,267
- Look, Philip,

1364
00:55:48,267 --> 00:55:49,433
there are many galaxies
- The fish.

1365
00:55:49,433 --> 00:55:50,667
- besides ours.

1366
00:55:50,667 --> 00:55:52,867
Now, who knows what
is happening millions

1367
00:55:52,867 --> 00:55:54,667
of miles out in space.

1368
00:55:54,667 --> 00:55:56,133
- Sagan knows.
- Perhaps the world

1369
00:55:56,133 --> 00:55:59,500
that these creatures
inhabit is coming to an end.

1370
00:55:59,500 --> 00:56:01,733
Perhaps they need to find
somewhere else to live.

1371
00:56:01,733 --> 00:56:03,100
- Well why pick on us?

1372
00:56:03,100 --> 00:56:05,367
- We don't know it's just us.

1373
00:56:05,367 --> 00:56:08,567
Maybe it's also on Mars,
Jupiter, Venus, who can tell.

1374
00:56:10,467 --> 00:56:12,600
- Do you go along with this?

1375
00:56:12,600 --> 00:56:15,667
- Until somebody comes up
with a better theory, yes.

1376
00:56:15,667 --> 00:56:17,767
- Or a sitcom.
- Yes, I do.

1377
00:56:17,767 --> 00:56:21,400
- Alright, alright,
while we're theorizing,

1378
00:56:21,400 --> 00:56:23,933
why, do you think,
do they always happen

1379
00:56:23,933 --> 00:56:25,733
to land on the top of mountains?

1380
00:56:25,733 --> 00:56:28,633
First the Andes, and
now the Trullenberg.

1381
00:56:28,633 --> 00:56:30,667
- Well, it's the
atmosphere, I think.

1382
00:56:30,667 --> 00:56:34,100
You see, it gets
much thinner high up.

1383
00:56:34,100 --> 00:56:36,767
Perhaps these
creatures need that.

1384
00:56:36,767 --> 00:56:39,400
The cloud would seem
to indicate it too.

1385
00:56:39,400 --> 00:56:41,467
Creating their own atmosphere.

1386
00:56:41,467 --> 00:56:43,500
- Well if they can only exist
on the top of mountains,

1387
00:56:43,500 --> 00:56:44,533
they are hardly likely to
inherit the earth, are they?

1388
00:56:46,067 --> 00:56:48,667
- You see, anyone can get
used to anything in time.

1389
00:56:49,600 --> 00:56:50,833
- [Tom] Like I, you.

1390
00:56:50,833 --> 00:56:53,267
- Now this movement
we have recorded here,

1391
00:56:53,267 --> 00:56:56,500
each time lower
down the mountain.

1392
00:56:56,500 --> 00:56:57,333
The climatization flex.

1393
00:56:58,567 --> 00:57:00,067
- What next, Professor?

1394
00:57:00,067 --> 00:57:02,433
- The next move
is up to you Alan.

1395
00:57:02,433 --> 00:57:05,567
You must inform the authorities.

1396
00:57:05,567 --> 00:57:07,033
- [Crow] Or the authorities.

1397
00:57:07,033 --> 00:57:09,133
- Have you seen the professor?

1398
00:57:09,133 --> 00:57:10,400
- He's in the office
with Alan Brooks,

1399
00:57:10,400 --> 00:57:11,767
they're telephoning Zurich.

1400
00:57:13,167 --> 00:57:14,733
- Any sign of Mr. Brett yet?

1401
00:57:14,733 --> 00:57:17,433
- Well the plane spotted
someone he thought was human,

1402
00:57:17,433 --> 00:57:19,700
two of the searchers
went to meet him.

1403
00:57:19,700 --> 00:57:22,200
The others are betting
they'll see nothing.

1404
00:57:22,200 --> 00:57:24,833
- Well if they won the bet,
they oughta be back by now.

1405
00:57:28,033 --> 00:57:29,000
- Where's your sister?

1406
00:57:29,000 --> 00:57:30,467
- She's in her room.

1407
00:57:30,467 --> 00:57:31,933
- Good.

1408
00:57:31,933 --> 00:57:35,100
- You think she's safe
from our friends up there?

1409
00:57:35,100 --> 00:57:37,033
- Well, I don't think they'll
try the same thing again.

1410
00:57:37,033 --> 00:57:40,600
The trouble is,
what will they try?

1411
00:57:43,300 --> 00:57:47,333
(whistling)
(imitating western music)

1412
00:57:47,333 --> 00:57:50,200
- [Crow] Wow, he looks
dead on his feet.

1413
00:57:50,200 --> 00:57:53,867
- A bit tired,
otherwise alright.

1414
00:57:53,867 --> 00:57:55,067
- Where have you been?

1415
00:57:55,067 --> 00:57:57,033
We've had search
parties out for you.

1416
00:57:57,033 --> 00:57:57,867
- I was lost.

1417
00:57:59,300 --> 00:58:00,900
- [Tom] In a fog, if you will.

1418
00:58:00,900 --> 00:58:03,400
- Dewhurst get back alright?
- When did you see him last?

1419
00:58:04,700 --> 00:58:08,733
- Dewhurst?
- Yes.

1420
00:58:08,733 --> 00:58:10,267
- Last night, I saw him.

1421
00:58:10,267 --> 00:58:11,333
- At the hut?

1422
00:58:12,467 --> 00:58:13,900
- Yes at the hut.

1423
00:58:20,033 --> 00:58:21,433
It's a bit hot in here.

1424
00:58:23,267 --> 00:58:24,433
Pour me a drink, Klein.

1425
00:58:29,800 --> 00:58:30,867
- Have one on me.

1426
00:58:37,867 --> 00:58:39,900
- [Sarah] Let me help you.

1427
00:58:39,900 --> 00:58:40,733
- Sorry.

1428
00:58:43,467 --> 00:58:44,300
Thank you.

1429
00:58:46,200 --> 00:58:48,000
- [Crow] He can't
handle his liquor.

1430
00:58:53,300 --> 00:58:55,733
- How's your sister,
Miss Pilgrim?

1431
00:58:55,733 --> 00:58:57,533
- She's all right now.

1432
00:58:57,533 --> 00:58:59,233
- What do they think?

1433
00:58:59,233 --> 00:59:00,900
- She's in--
- Here,

1434
00:59:00,900 --> 00:59:01,967
have a cigarette.

1435
00:59:03,400 --> 00:59:04,933
- [Tom] Great, we saw how
well he did with the booze.

1436
00:59:04,933 --> 00:59:06,800
Now let's give him
something that's lit.

1437
00:59:15,567 --> 00:59:19,467
429 matches exactly,
exactly 429.

1438
00:59:20,600 --> 00:59:21,900
- [Joel] That's a good
impression, Servo.

1439
00:59:21,900 --> 00:59:23,400
- [Tom] I'm an excellent driver.

1440
00:59:23,400 --> 00:59:24,833
(Joel chuckling)

1441
00:59:24,833 --> 00:59:27,133
- [Crow] He's gonna
light his nose, look out.

1442
00:59:27,133 --> 00:59:28,333
- [Joel] What are
they gonna do next,

1443
00:59:28,333 --> 00:59:30,033
have him drive a
fork lift, you guys?

1444
00:59:30,033 --> 00:59:31,300
Jeez.

1445
00:59:31,300 --> 00:59:35,167
Glutton for punishing
others. (chuckling)

1446
00:59:39,100 --> 00:59:41,200
- [Crow] Let's get
the dog drunk next.

1447
00:59:41,200 --> 00:59:42,867
(chuckling)

1448
00:59:42,867 --> 00:59:44,567
- It's too scary up
there on your own.

1449
00:59:44,567 --> 00:59:46,000
- Were you all on your own?

1450
00:59:48,267 --> 00:59:51,133
- After I lost Dewhurst--

1451
00:59:51,133 --> 00:59:53,267
- I'm sorry to be a
nuisance, but may I--

1452
00:59:53,267 --> 00:59:55,233
(dramatic music)

1453
00:59:55,233 --> 00:59:56,633
- [Joel] Then she walked in.

1454
01:00:01,500 --> 01:00:03,867
Ooh, owie.
- He's kinda pig headed.

1455
01:00:04,800 --> 01:00:05,833
- [Crow] That smarts.

1456
01:00:07,733 --> 01:00:08,667
- Look at his head.

1457
01:00:08,667 --> 01:00:09,767
- [Crow] At least he's got one.

1458
01:00:11,367 --> 01:00:13,100
- But, there's no blood.

1459
01:00:14,300 --> 01:00:16,200
- [Tom] He's wearing
Mr. Spock's jammies.

1460
01:00:17,967 --> 01:00:19,800
- [Joel] Dig a little deeper.

1461
01:00:19,800 --> 01:00:22,500
- [Tom] Yeah, that's a good
idea, solder him back together.

1462
01:00:24,033 --> 01:00:25,767
- There, that should
take care of him

1463
01:00:25,767 --> 01:00:27,800
until the morning, I think.

1464
01:00:27,800 --> 01:00:29,333
- Good.

1465
01:00:29,333 --> 01:00:30,533
- [Crow] He should be up and
spilling liquor in no time.

1466
01:00:30,533 --> 01:00:32,333
- Dr. Becker can
have a look at him.

1467
01:00:33,800 --> 01:00:36,733
- Do we tell the others
what he was trying to do?

1468
01:00:39,300 --> 01:00:40,300
- I don't know.

1469
01:00:46,000 --> 01:00:46,833
- Looks great.

1470
01:00:51,167 --> 01:00:52,233
- [Crow] Let's lock him in here.

1471
01:00:52,233 --> 01:00:53,233
- I could use a drink.

1472
01:00:53,233 --> 01:00:54,667
- [Joel] So what else is new?

1473
01:00:57,733 --> 01:01:00,467
- [Joel] I think we
all could use a drink.

1474
01:01:00,467 --> 01:01:03,000
(upbeat music)

1475
01:01:06,800 --> 01:01:09,300
(dramatic music)

1476
01:01:09,300 --> 01:01:11,300
- [Crow] Who put this
zipper in my head?

1477
01:01:18,533 --> 01:01:21,367
Wow, what a party,
I'm dead tired.

1478
01:01:26,867 --> 01:01:28,767
A little thirsty
though, I need a drink.

1479
01:01:28,767 --> 01:01:30,067
Maybe some formaldehyde.

1480
01:01:31,400 --> 01:01:32,967
Hope they serve the dead here.

1481
01:01:47,833 --> 01:01:49,100
- [Joel] Looks like Popeye.

1482
01:01:49,100 --> 01:01:50,567
(Tom imitating Popeye's laugh)

1483
01:01:50,567 --> 01:01:53,800
- [Tom] Oh, somebody's coming
down the hallways, geh.

1484
01:01:57,333 --> 01:02:00,333
- [Joel] Doesn't look
like anybody's in there.

1485
01:02:00,333 --> 01:02:03,733
Uh oh, I'll see about
that. (groaning)

1486
01:02:03,733 --> 01:02:06,600
- [Tom] Eh, I think I'll reach
through and get hims, geh.

1487
01:02:06,600 --> 01:02:08,400
- [Joel] Oh, hey,
Popeye, ooh, oh.

1488
01:02:08,400 --> 01:02:10,767
♪ I'm Popeye the sailor man ♪

1489
01:02:10,767 --> 01:02:13,233
♪ I've got a guy's
head in my hand ♪

1490
01:02:13,233 --> 01:02:16,000
(dramatic music)

1491
01:02:16,000 --> 01:02:17,800
- [Joel] This legal
submission hold.

1492
01:02:19,233 --> 01:02:20,933
- [Crow] Sounds like a bad
Popeye impression to me.

1493
01:02:20,933 --> 01:02:23,200
(grunting)

1494
01:02:27,300 --> 01:02:30,400
- [Joel] I think you
proved your point.

1495
01:02:30,400 --> 01:02:31,933
- [Tom] Dropped him
like a bad habit.

1496
01:02:31,933 --> 01:02:33,600
- [Crow] He's just
misunderstood.

1497
01:02:36,733 --> 01:02:38,200
- [Tom] Most dead people are.

1498
01:02:44,967 --> 01:02:47,533
- [Joel] He'll make
an excellent puppet.

1499
01:02:47,533 --> 01:02:48,567
There's Ward Cleaver.

1500
01:02:50,000 --> 01:02:53,133
- [Crow] What do ya think
he's gonna do with that?

1501
01:02:53,133 --> 01:02:56,100
(suspenseful music)

1502
01:03:13,567 --> 01:03:14,800
Mind if I cut in?

1503
01:03:17,167 --> 01:03:18,700
- [Tom] Here's Johnny.

1504
01:03:18,700 --> 01:03:20,767
Wendy, I'm home.

1505
01:03:34,267 --> 01:03:35,633
Pretty lady?

1506
01:03:37,600 --> 01:03:39,100
- Alan.

1507
01:03:39,100 --> 01:03:40,433
- [Crow] Norman, I told you
not to bother the guests.

1508
01:03:40,433 --> 01:03:41,767
- Alan, oh Alan.

1509
01:03:43,833 --> 01:03:45,467
- [Tom] Oh, my.

1510
01:03:45,467 --> 01:03:47,900
- Alan, Alan, wake up please.

1511
01:03:49,267 --> 01:03:51,933
Alan please, Alan, come quickly.

1512
01:03:57,300 --> 01:03:59,167
(screaming)
(gun fires)

1513
01:03:59,167 --> 01:04:02,200
- [Tom] And that's for
killing the fat guy.

1514
01:04:02,200 --> 01:04:04,800
- [Crow] Whoa, carries
a pistol in his pajamas.

1515
01:04:06,233 --> 01:04:09,767
- [Joel] What do you think
that pocket's for, Kleenex?

1516
01:04:09,767 --> 01:04:11,200
- Look at this.
- What?

1517
01:04:12,100 --> 01:04:14,333
- Hans, bring the light closer.

1518
01:04:14,333 --> 01:04:15,800
- [Tom] Lower, lower.

1519
01:04:15,800 --> 01:04:16,933
- Lower.
- Lower!

1520
01:04:16,933 --> 01:04:17,933
- Put it on the floor.

1521
01:04:17,933 --> 01:04:19,333
- Lower.
- Even lower.

1522
01:04:19,333 --> 01:04:22,467
- See the way the flesh
reflects the light?

1523
01:04:23,300 --> 01:04:24,567
- [Joel] Pretty.

1524
01:04:24,567 --> 01:04:25,800
- Almost as though
it's crystallized.

1525
01:04:27,867 --> 01:04:30,333
Like meat that's
been deep frozen.

1526
01:04:30,333 --> 01:04:31,800
- I still don't
understand how such a--

1527
01:04:31,800 --> 01:04:33,233
(yelping)

1528
01:04:33,233 --> 01:04:34,967
- [Crow] Wow, unfortunate skin.

1529
01:04:36,067 --> 01:04:38,233
(gasping and gagging)

1530
01:04:38,233 --> 01:04:39,733
- [Joel] Did we
have to see that?

1531
01:04:39,733 --> 01:04:41,700
- Alan, the observatory's
not been on.

1532
01:04:41,700 --> 01:04:43,067
That cloud is moving again.

1533
01:04:43,067 --> 01:04:44,467
- [Tom] Well, we haven't
seen the monster yet.

1534
01:04:44,467 --> 01:04:45,833
It's good to see something.

1535
01:04:47,667 --> 01:04:49,133
- [Joel] It's coming.

1536
01:04:49,133 --> 01:04:49,933
- You're wasting your
time, that phone is dead.

1537
01:04:49,933 --> 01:04:51,433
The wires have been cut.

1538
01:04:51,433 --> 01:04:52,900
We gotta get up to
the observatory.

1539
01:04:52,900 --> 01:04:54,100
- But we must--

1540
01:04:54,100 --> 01:04:55,033
- You heard what
the professor said,

1541
01:04:55,033 --> 01:04:56,500
that place is built like a fort.

1542
01:04:56,500 --> 01:04:58,033
If we're gonna have any direct
contact with these things

1543
01:04:58,033 --> 01:04:59,967
at least we'll have some
kind of chance up there.

1544
01:04:59,967 --> 01:05:02,733
- It's insane, we should try
to get out to the valley.

1545
01:05:02,733 --> 01:05:06,300
- The road is blocked, the
cloud stretches right across it.

1546
01:05:06,300 --> 01:05:08,067
- [Joel] Now go out and
do something irrational.

1547
01:05:08,067 --> 01:05:10,867
- Yes, yes, you go out on the
next cable car with the girls.

1548
01:05:11,867 --> 01:05:12,867
- [Tom] Hey, dudes.

1549
01:05:12,867 --> 01:05:14,167
- You got everyone?

1550
01:05:14,167 --> 01:05:16,000
- [Man] Not everyone,
we've got 12 left.

1551
01:05:16,000 --> 01:05:17,267
One car's already gone
up, and the other one

1552
01:05:17,267 --> 01:05:18,900
will be down in 10 minutes.

1553
01:05:20,533 --> 01:05:22,567
(car revving)

1554
01:05:22,567 --> 01:05:23,400
- Who is that?

1555
01:05:23,400 --> 01:05:24,233
- Hey.
- Hans.

1556
01:05:27,200 --> 01:05:29,033
- [Tom] Hey Hans, get some ice.

1557
01:05:29,033 --> 01:05:31,533
(car revving)

1558
01:05:35,033 --> 01:05:35,933
Poor fool.

1559
01:05:37,367 --> 01:05:38,900
- Alright everybody, let's
get up to the cable car

1560
01:05:38,900 --> 01:05:39,833
as quickly as we can.

1561
01:05:39,833 --> 01:05:40,900
We haven't got very much time.

1562
01:05:40,900 --> 01:05:42,400
- [Crow] Oh really?

1563
01:05:42,400 --> 01:05:43,733
I thought we'd just hang
around for an hour or two.

1564
01:05:43,733 --> 01:05:46,467
(dramatic music)

1565
01:05:48,533 --> 01:05:49,767
- [Joel] Uh oh.

1566
01:05:50,967 --> 01:05:51,833
- What about Hans?

1567
01:05:51,833 --> 01:05:53,033
- [Crow] I bet they'll

1568
01:05:53,033 --> 01:05:53,700
be back for that.
- He's driving through

1569
01:05:53,700 --> 01:05:54,333
the cloud.

1570
01:05:56,300 --> 01:05:58,000
- [Joel] I didn't
know they got cable.

1571
01:05:59,100 --> 01:06:00,300
- [Crow] Both channels.

1572
01:06:05,267 --> 01:06:07,200
- [Tom] If we only had a bomb.

1573
01:06:07,200 --> 01:06:10,867
A small rubber bomb,
then we'd be fine.

1574
01:06:10,867 --> 01:06:12,433
- [Crow] Wait, I think I
know where we can find one.

1575
01:06:12,433 --> 01:06:14,133
- Professor, are you sure
we're doing the right thing

1576
01:06:14,133 --> 01:06:15,767
coming up to the observatory?

1577
01:06:15,767 --> 01:06:17,633
- (speaking foreign
language) I don't know.

1578
01:06:17,633 --> 01:06:20,700
What I do know is, it's wrong
to stay down in the village.

1579
01:06:20,700 --> 01:06:23,500
Up there, we have
some chance at least.

1580
01:06:23,500 --> 01:06:25,900
- Professor, look,
on the lower slopes.

1581
01:06:25,900 --> 01:06:28,000
The cloud, it's moving faster.

1582
01:06:31,633 --> 01:06:33,333
- I hope they see it down there.

1583
01:06:37,900 --> 01:06:39,900
- The operator says it
is five minutes now.

1584
01:06:39,900 --> 01:06:41,300
- Good.

1585
01:06:41,300 --> 01:06:42,200
- Alan.

1586
01:06:45,867 --> 01:06:47,667
The cloud's moving
pretty fast now.

1587
01:06:47,667 --> 01:06:50,433
(dramatic music)

1588
01:06:53,833 --> 01:06:55,867
- Please folks, I'd
like you all to be ready

1589
01:06:55,867 --> 01:06:58,033
to load in the car as
soon as it gets down.

1590
01:06:58,033 --> 01:06:59,700
We haven't got a
second to spare.

1591
01:07:01,200 --> 01:07:03,733
- [Tom] Why don't you just
spew out more obvious facts?

1592
01:07:05,133 --> 01:07:07,233
- At least we'd know what
we we're dealing with.

1593
01:07:07,233 --> 01:07:09,800
- Well, we'll know soon enough.

1594
01:07:10,933 --> 01:07:13,433
- [Crow] Wow, is that
tastelessly done.

1595
01:07:13,433 --> 01:07:15,533
It's such a mish-mash of styles.

1596
01:07:17,000 --> 01:07:18,767
- [Joel] Kinda
garish all around.

1597
01:07:18,767 --> 01:07:20,467
- [Crow] Who
decorated this joint?

1598
01:07:22,533 --> 01:07:24,900
- [Tom] Someone with a
really big butt sat there.

1599
01:07:28,600 --> 01:07:30,033
Is that the crawling eye, Joel?

1600
01:07:30,033 --> 01:07:32,100
- [Joel] No, that's
the little ball.

1601
01:07:41,400 --> 01:07:43,200
See now, this is where
your weatherizing

1602
01:07:43,200 --> 01:07:44,200
would really pay off.

1603
01:07:44,200 --> 01:07:45,800
They're probably
losing up to 70%

1604
01:07:45,800 --> 01:07:47,333
of their heat out that door.

1605
01:07:47,333 --> 01:07:48,867
- Seems to have
stopped at the hotel.

1606
01:07:50,333 --> 01:07:51,867
- Yeah, well, as long as it
stays there, we're alright.

1607
01:07:51,867 --> 01:07:54,500
At least that cloud lets
us see where it moves.

1608
01:07:59,000 --> 01:08:01,733
Let's get aboard as
quickly as possible please.

1609
01:08:01,733 --> 01:08:03,900
That's it, there you are.

1610
01:08:03,900 --> 01:08:05,333
Please mam, please.

1611
01:08:07,233 --> 01:08:08,300
- My kid, where's my kid?

1612
01:08:10,000 --> 01:08:11,500
- Of course, the child.

1613
01:08:11,500 --> 01:08:14,233
(dramatic music)

1614
01:08:15,667 --> 01:08:16,900
- If that cloud starts to move
this was before I get back,

1615
01:08:16,900 --> 01:08:18,867
take the car up,
do you understand?

1616
01:08:18,867 --> 01:08:21,967
- What about you?
- Do as you're told.

1617
01:08:26,333 --> 01:08:28,300
- Whoa!
- Holy, what the?

1618
01:08:28,300 --> 01:08:30,300
- Look at that!
- Ah, jeez.

1619
01:08:31,467 --> 01:08:32,967
- [Tom] I'm on the
edge of my seat.

1620
01:08:32,967 --> 01:08:35,267
- [Joel] He thinks that
ball's one of his pupils.

1621
01:08:35,267 --> 01:08:38,300
- [Tom] (laughing) Stop
it, you're killing me.

1622
01:08:39,567 --> 01:08:41,400
Ooh, look at that
milky discharge.

1623
01:08:42,833 --> 01:08:44,933
I think he has conjunctivitis.

1624
01:08:44,933 --> 01:08:47,533
(child crying)

1625
01:08:49,600 --> 01:08:51,367
- [Joel] Now look here, you.

1626
01:08:53,133 --> 01:08:54,167
Give you a trim.

1627
01:08:57,800 --> 01:08:59,733
- [Crow] Hey wait,
what about the ball?

1628
01:09:00,867 --> 01:09:04,533
(speaking foreign language)

1629
01:09:10,200 --> 01:09:11,667
- [Tom] Hey.

1630
01:09:11,667 --> 01:09:12,833
- Get in the cable car.

1631
01:09:13,833 --> 01:09:15,467
That's it.

1632
01:09:15,467 --> 01:09:17,200
- [Tom] Tickets, everyone?

1633
01:09:18,133 --> 01:09:19,633
- [Joel] I don't need one.

1634
01:09:19,633 --> 01:09:21,000
- [Tom] And Forrest Tucker.

1635
01:09:25,933 --> 01:09:28,333
- [Tom] Oh look, it fell
off its fabric softener box.

1636
01:09:28,333 --> 01:09:30,533
- [Crow] Now they gotta
go back for the bear.

1637
01:09:31,700 --> 01:09:32,967
- [Joel] Oh, we
better get outta here.

1638
01:09:32,967 --> 01:09:34,900
- I'm outta here.
- Carry me, honey.

1639
01:09:39,633 --> 01:09:42,367
- That was a close shave.

1640
01:09:42,367 --> 01:09:44,367
- We're not through yet.

1641
01:09:46,067 --> 01:09:51,067
(doors clanking and slamming)
(whirring)

1642
01:09:54,300 --> 01:09:57,200
- Oh, Joel, it
was so horrifying.

1643
01:09:57,200 --> 01:09:59,067
- Yeah, really, you think so?

1644
01:09:59,067 --> 01:10:00,933
- So ugly, so hideous.

1645
01:10:00,933 --> 01:10:03,700
- Oh yeah, that was
some eye, wasn't it?

1646
01:10:03,700 --> 01:10:06,367
- No, not the eye, we're
talking about Forrest Tucker.

1647
01:10:06,367 --> 01:10:09,533
- Oh yeah, all that
vitalis, those prop glasses,

1648
01:10:09,533 --> 01:10:13,467
and those heavy,
dramatic pauses.

1649
01:10:13,467 --> 01:10:15,300
I'm drained.
- No, you guys,

1650
01:10:15,300 --> 01:10:17,000
no, you got it wrong, you see.

1651
01:10:17,000 --> 01:10:19,600
The eye is what's horrible about
this movie, think about it.

1652
01:10:19,600 --> 01:10:22,633
Otherwise it woulda been called
The Crawling Forrest Tucker.

1653
01:10:22,633 --> 01:10:23,867
- I guess.

1654
01:10:23,867 --> 01:10:26,533
Joel, what's so scary
about a big eye anyway?

1655
01:10:26,533 --> 01:10:29,500
- Oh, I see, well that's
another human being thing.

1656
01:10:29,500 --> 01:10:31,133
You know, any time
we're confronted

1657
01:10:31,133 --> 01:10:34,467
with some appendage of
ours that's disconnected

1658
01:10:34,467 --> 01:10:37,800
and free-roaming, rogue, you
know, genetically bloated

1659
01:10:37,800 --> 01:10:41,233
to 10 times its normal size,
we're automatically scared.

1660
01:10:41,233 --> 01:10:43,067
- Especially if
they run in packs.

1661
01:10:43,067 --> 01:10:44,433
- Oh-ho, I know what you mean.

1662
01:10:44,433 --> 01:10:45,800
Once I got hassled by a pack

1663
01:10:45,800 --> 01:10:48,267
of really tough
pituitary glands.

1664
01:10:48,267 --> 01:10:50,167
And you know how
immature they can be.

1665
01:10:50,167 --> 01:10:52,067
- I still don't see
why it's so scary.

1666
01:10:52,067 --> 01:10:54,333
You can just walk up to
it and throw salt at it.

1667
01:10:54,333 --> 01:10:56,100
- Or just a squeeze
of lemon juice.

1668
01:10:56,100 --> 01:10:58,167
- Right, I know, it's
kind of a plot hole,

1669
01:10:58,167 --> 01:10:59,400
but let's kinda look it over.

1670
01:10:59,400 --> 01:11:01,633
You see these, okay,
these giant eyes

1671
01:11:01,633 --> 01:11:05,000
come from a world that's
designed for 'em, you know.

1672
01:11:05,000 --> 01:11:07,800
It's completely compatible,
like their highways

1673
01:11:07,800 --> 01:11:10,933
are made outta Nerf
and they don't have

1674
01:11:10,933 --> 01:11:12,333
to take shop classes.

1675
01:11:12,333 --> 01:11:13,967
- And they only
use baby shampoo?

1676
01:11:13,967 --> 01:11:16,500
- Right, right
exactly, so anyway.

1677
01:11:16,500 --> 01:11:18,667
They come down from this
planet and they decide

1678
01:11:18,667 --> 01:11:20,133
to land on a mountain peak.

1679
01:11:20,133 --> 01:11:22,467
- Which is really stupid,
'cause as we all know,

1680
01:11:22,467 --> 01:11:25,833
a giant sharp point is the
giant eye's natural enemy.

1681
01:11:25,833 --> 01:11:27,633
- Bingo, so anyway.

1682
01:11:27,633 --> 01:11:31,867
They come down and they fouled
up their atmosphere somehow,

1683
01:11:31,867 --> 01:11:34,100
I don't know, it's hay
fever season or something,

1684
01:11:34,100 --> 01:11:35,800
and they figure they'd
come down to Earth.

1685
01:11:35,800 --> 01:11:38,167
We'll have our first
frost already done with

1686
01:11:38,167 --> 01:11:40,167
so they come down and
they have no concept

1687
01:11:40,167 --> 01:11:42,967
of anything at all like
wearing safety glasses

1688
01:11:42,967 --> 01:11:45,167
or protective
eyewear of any kind.

1689
01:11:45,167 --> 01:11:47,033
- Joel, I think we've
already spent more time

1690
01:11:47,033 --> 01:11:50,300
examining this plot than
the writers ever did.

1691
01:11:50,300 --> 01:11:52,700
- Now here's something
you'll really like.

1692
01:11:52,700 --> 01:11:55,267
(upbeat music)

1693
01:12:02,433 --> 01:12:03,400
- [Joel] This is the last time.

1694
01:12:03,400 --> 01:12:05,067
- [Tom] Put me down.

1695
01:12:13,133 --> 01:12:16,167
As a fog emerges
over Trullenberg,

1696
01:12:16,167 --> 01:12:17,667
our young group escapes.

1697
01:12:19,133 --> 01:12:21,633
- [Joel] Looks like
everything's free and clear now.

1698
01:12:22,800 --> 01:12:26,667
Nothing to do but
to get up the rope.

1699
01:12:26,667 --> 01:12:28,667
- [Tom] Sit back and relax.

1700
01:12:28,667 --> 01:12:30,267
- [Tom And Joel] Enjoy the ride.

1701
01:12:32,633 --> 01:12:36,967
- [Tom] Uh oh, maybe
we spoke too soon.

1702
01:12:36,967 --> 01:12:38,500
- [Joel] Oh, now they're
gonna have to wait all day

1703
01:12:38,500 --> 01:12:40,567
for that cable guy to show up.

1704
01:12:40,567 --> 01:12:42,333
- This contraption operate
in the extreme cold?

1705
01:12:42,333 --> 01:12:44,133
- We've worked the
cable car at 30 below.

1706
01:12:44,133 --> 01:12:45,367
- Colder than that?

1707
01:12:45,367 --> 01:12:46,833
- [Crow] Colder than a winch.

1708
01:12:49,600 --> 01:12:51,767
- [Joel] Tell scary stories.

1709
01:12:51,767 --> 01:12:52,567
- [Tom] Oh my.

1710
01:12:54,300 --> 01:12:55,167
- The cable!

1711
01:12:55,167 --> 01:12:55,967
It will snap.

1712
01:13:05,033 --> 01:13:06,400
- [Tom] Am I gonna have
time to change my pants

1713
01:13:06,400 --> 01:13:07,900
when we get there?

1714
01:13:07,900 --> 01:13:10,200
- [Joel] Oh funny, good one.

1715
01:13:15,000 --> 01:13:17,900
Tell 'em all about the
giant eye, Forrest.

1716
01:13:17,900 --> 01:13:19,533
- All right quick,
everybody out.

1717
01:13:22,767 --> 01:13:23,600
That's it.

1718
01:13:35,333 --> 01:13:36,800
- We're keeping the
villagers out here, sir.

1719
01:13:36,800 --> 01:13:38,700
The shop is safe and they
won't get in the way.

1720
01:13:38,700 --> 01:13:39,933
- Are the radios still working?

1721
01:13:39,933 --> 01:13:42,467
- We checked about
15 minutes ago, sir.

1722
01:13:42,467 --> 01:13:43,600
- Oh, Alan, come here.

1723
01:13:45,733 --> 01:13:46,867
Alan we are in big trouble.

1724
01:13:46,867 --> 01:13:48,200
- Well what now?

1725
01:13:48,200 --> 01:13:51,333
- The cloud is splitting up.
- Splitting up?

1726
01:13:51,333 --> 01:13:55,167
- There are four of them
now and all moving this way.

1727
01:13:55,167 --> 01:13:56,567
- [Crow] Four eyes.

1728
01:13:56,567 --> 01:13:57,400
- How long before they get here?

1729
01:13:57,400 --> 01:13:58,667
- An hour maybe.

1730
01:13:58,667 --> 01:13:59,733
- Then we've got just
an hour to decide

1731
01:13:59,733 --> 01:14:01,033
what we're gonna do.

1732
01:14:01,033 --> 01:14:02,800
You better check
that radio again.

1733
01:14:02,800 --> 01:14:04,200
- You're going to
signal the authorities?

1734
01:14:04,200 --> 01:14:05,333
- To tell them what?

1735
01:14:05,333 --> 01:14:06,400
What are used
against these things?

1736
01:14:06,400 --> 01:14:07,800
- High explosives?

1737
01:14:07,800 --> 01:14:09,400
- Bombs?

1738
01:14:09,400 --> 01:14:10,633
By the time the plane get here,

1739
01:14:10,633 --> 01:14:11,867
those things will
be all over us.

1740
01:14:11,867 --> 01:14:13,100
How do they drop
their bombs then?

1741
01:14:13,100 --> 01:14:15,000
- The third is still
working okay, sir.

1742
01:14:15,000 --> 01:14:18,600
- Good, lemme see
that thing again.

1743
01:14:18,600 --> 01:14:21,433
- Here's someone knocking at
the outside door, Herr Wilde.

1744
01:14:25,033 --> 01:14:26,367
- [Joel] It's Bob Hope.

1745
01:14:26,367 --> 01:14:27,967
- [Tom] Hey there,
join me this week

1746
01:14:27,967 --> 01:14:30,500
with Brooke Shields
and Erik Estrada.

1747
01:14:30,500 --> 01:14:32,333
